WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:09.120
- Welcome to the Yale
Law School auditorium

00:00:10.113 --> 00:00:11.263
and to Yale University.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.463
It's a distinct honor today,
my name is Peter Salovey,

00:00:15.463 --> 00:00:18.560
I'm the president of Yale,
and it's a distinct honor

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.690
to introduce our 2015 Chubb Fellow,

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:27.390
President Obama's national
security advisor, Susan E. Rice.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:31.070
Ambassador Rice, we welcome
you to Yale University

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:34.240
and we're so grateful for your willingness

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:38.600
to share your time and your
insights with all of us.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:42.940
We're delighted that Ambassador
Rice is joined today by

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:47.940
a member of her family who is
also a member of our family.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:52.333
This is John Rice, Yale
College class of 1988.

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:56.533
And a current fellow of
the Yale Corporation.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:01.340
Before I speak about our
visitors, I wanna say a few words

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:04.810
about the history of the Chubb Fellowship.

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:07.800
So Hendon Chubb was an 1895 graduate

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.560
of Yale's Sheffield Scientific School,

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.270
that's the precursor to the
Yale School of Engineering

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:15.780
and Applied Sciences.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:20.260
In 1936, he made a substantial
gift to the Yale University

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:25.260
to encourage students to take
an interest in public service.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.820
Since 1949, the Chubb
endowment has been used

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:34.170
to underwrite prestigious
visiting speakers

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:39.170
who would bring kind of
extraordinary presences to campus

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:43.030
that would inspire our students.

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:46.880
This fellowship is
administered by the master

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:50.313
of Timothy Dwight College, Mary Lui.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.920
Past Chubb Fellows have
included four presidents

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:58.960
of the United States, presidents
of eight other countries

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:02.670
and more than 150 other regional, national

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:05.350
and internationally prominent individuals

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:09.333
noted for their distinction
in service to the common good.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:14.680
The annual Chubb lecture
is a wonderful opportunity

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:19.020
to reinforce the shared
values of our community.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:22.720
And I know it's a special
treat today for you all

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:26.870
to hear from someone who is serving

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:31.160
at the very heart of our nation
and our national interest.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:35.030
As national security advisor,
Ambassador Rice oversees

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:37.390
the National Security Council staff,

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:40.520
chairs the National Security
Principles committee,

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:44.840
provides the president's daily
national security briefing

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:46.640
and is responsible for coordinating

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:48.770
the administration's foreign policy,

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:52.030
intelligence and military efforts.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:53.820
She has held numerous appointments within

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:57.350
the national security
council, including as director

00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:00.990
for international
organizations and peacekeeping,

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:03.970
as special assistant to President Clinton

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:08.870
and as senior director
for African affairs.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:13.100
From 1997 to 2001, she was
the US assistant secretary

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:16.450
of state for African affairs, and in 2000,

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:18.710
she was co-recipient of the White House's

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:20.560
Samuel Nelson Drew Memorial Award

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:23.550
for distinguished
contributions to the formation

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:27.093
of peaceful cooperative
relationships between states.

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:32.000
As the senior advisor for
national security affairs

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:36.130
with the Obama for America campaign,

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:39.450
she went on to serve as a
member of the advisory board

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:43.420
for the Obama/Biden transition,
and then as co-chair

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:46.450
of its policy working
group on national security.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:50.460
From 2002 to 2009, she was a senior fellow

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:53.040
at the Brookings Institution,
where she focused

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.980
on US foreign policy,
transnational security threats,

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:00.823
weak states, global
poverty and development.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:06.050
Before her appointment as
national security advisor in 2013,

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:08.850
she served as the US
permanent representative

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:11.130
to the United Nations, where she worked

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:14.020
to strengthen national and global security

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:17.000
and to promote human rights.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.610
Ambassador Rice received her BA in history

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:21.510
from Stanford University.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:24.670
She holds an MPhil and DPhil
in international relations

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:27.540
from New College at Oxford University.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:30.600
And she was a Rhodes Scholar there.

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:34.900
This is a remarkable
opportunity for our students

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:37.590
not just to learn about
issues in national security,

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:41.970
but to learn about a career
path in public service

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:45.860
that I am sure is an inspiration.

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:48.300
I'm particularly pleased
that today's lecture

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:51.260
will actually take the
form of a conversation.

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:53.530
And it's a conversation
between Ambassador Rice

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:57.840
and as I hinted at earlier, a
member of the Yale community

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:02.840
who is uniquely positioned
to join her here on stage.

00:05:02.890 --> 00:05:07.090
Why do I say that, well,
John Rice, Yale College '88,

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:10.120
and a fellow of the Yale
Corporation, is the founder

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:13.620
and CEO of Management
Leadership for Tomorrow,

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:15.630
which is a non-profit organization

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:18.780
that helps members of
underrepresented minority groups

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:20.980
to become leaders in the corporate,

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:24.290
non-profit and entrepreneurial sectors.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:26.730
After graduating from Yale with a degree

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:29.410
in Latin American
studies, he earned his MBA

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:32.910
from the Harvard Business
School, and while at Harvard,

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:35.730
he wrote a business plan
for his current organization

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:40.730
and in 1994, he launched
it via a mentoring program

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:43.640
for minority undergraduates.

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:46.890
He was appointed to President
Obama's Advisory Commission

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:49.830
on Educational Excellence
for African Americans

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:51.830
and has served as a member
of the President's board

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:56.670
of advisors on historically
black colleges and universities.

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:59.400
He also serves on a number
of non-profit boards

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:01.750
including LIFT, the
National Center for Civil

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:04.930
and Human Rights, the American
Management Association,

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:08.423
the Woodrow Wilson National
Fellowship Foundation.

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:13.450
As I mentioned, he is a member
of the Yale Corporation,

00:06:13.450 --> 00:06:16.860
the trustees of the Yale
University, our fiduciaries.

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:19.610
Before that, he was a member
of the University Council,

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:21.913
which is an advisory
group to the president.

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:26.310
But most importantly for today's purposes,

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:28.630
he holds a very special role and that is

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:31.450
he is Ambassador Rice's brother.

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:32.630
- Younger brother.

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:33.644
- So.

00:06:33.644 --> 00:06:34.700
(audience laughter)

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:37.750
- So I would like you to
join me in thanking him

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:40.300
for facilitating today's conversation

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:43.340
and to also join me in extending

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:47.300
a very warm welcome to
Connecticut, to New Haven,

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:50.856
to Yale University, to
Ambassador Susan E. Rice.

00:06:50.856 --> 00:06:51.689
- Thank you.

00:06:51.689 --> 00:06:53.939
(applause)

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:15.190
I feel like I need a phone book.

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:16.516
Can you all see me?

00:07:16.516 --> 00:07:17.970
(audience laughter)

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:19.970
Johnny got all the height in the family.

00:07:20.936 --> 00:07:23.250
- She likes to say that
I'm the younger brother

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:25.410
because she feels that she looks younger.

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:28.060
So that's a side to that.

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:30.620
So thanks for having us, first of all.

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:33.530
And I think we're gonna
try to have some fun

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:37.540
over the course of the hour here.

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:39.990
And as Peter said, approach
this a little bit differently

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:44.323
than potentially some of the
past Chubb Fellow lectures.

00:07:45.971 --> 00:07:48.830
What we'd like to do, and
again, I'll say, you know,

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:51.840
Susan and I have had plenty
of fireside chats over

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:54.577
the years, but none in public, so this is

00:07:54.577 --> 00:07:58.050
the first time we've done this
and bear with us, all right.

00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:00.460
So who knows where it will go, but we hope

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:01.580
it will be instructive.

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:05.430
And I think we'd like to
structure it in a way that

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:09.170
it's a little bit different
than if this were a, you know,

00:08:09.170 --> 00:08:12.060
if I were someone like a real interviewer,

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:14.590
someone who does this for a
living who happens, you know,

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:16.739
and because I'm her brother, hopefully,

00:08:16.739 --> 00:08:19.840
we can push a little bit
further on some issues

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:22.290
if she'll let me, get a
little bit more personal.

00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:26.320
And hopefully, we'll keep it professional.

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:27.690
- And hopefully, we'll
still like each other

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:28.523
when we're done.

00:08:28.523 --> 00:08:29.490
- Right, so...

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:30.360
(audience laughter)

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:32.999
So the way we're gonna structure this

00:08:32.999 --> 00:08:34.660
in terms of the fireside
chat, before we open it up

00:08:34.660 --> 00:08:38.030
for questions from you all,
is we'll talk a little bit

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:41.200
about the core here, you
know, sort of foreign policy

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:45.880
which is, you know, you're
living and breathing every day.

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:47.570
And then I think we'd like to broaden it

00:08:47.570 --> 00:08:50.160
and talk more generally
about issues that I think,

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:54.604
that I know that Susan
cares about that cut across,

00:08:54.604 --> 00:08:56.460
they may incorporate foreign policy,

00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:58.990
but they may be focused on other issues

00:08:58.990 --> 00:09:00.370
that she thinks are important.

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:02.170
I wanna hear her thoughts
on those (mumbles)

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:03.100
a few themes there.

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:05.930
And then we wanna give you more personal

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:08.830
and give you all a sense
of what makes her tick.

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:13.820
And hopefully, to share some
advice and perspective from

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:16.130
the, you know, the
experiences that she's had.

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:18.010
So that's gonna be the
approach, and obviously,

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:18.940
we'll open it up for questions

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:20.990
and we can go anywhere with
those questions as well.

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:22.040
Is that okay, Susan?

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:22.873
- No.

00:09:22.873 --> 00:09:23.953
- No, all right.

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:26.670
- It is what it is, go ahead.

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:28.353
- You might wanna pour
some water there too.

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:32.460
This is something she'd
never done, she's never,

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:33.980
you know, pouring some water for me to--

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:37.168
(audience laughter)

00:09:37.168 --> 00:09:39.990
But that's actually not
true, she's a great cook.

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:41.450
And she'd had us over for
dinner on Sunday night

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:44.680
and did a fantastic
job, so appreciate that.

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:46.720
(audience laughter)

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:49.720
So let's start foreign policy.

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:54.720
We know that she has a high
pressure job, and to say

00:09:56.090 --> 00:09:58.960
the least, one of the
highest pressure cooker jobs

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:00.260
I think in the world.

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:03.530
And so my first question
really is, you know, when,

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:07.533
describe a time where you
have been the most nervous.

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:12.010
When you worried the most about having to

00:10:12.010 --> 00:10:14.333
go do something before you did it.

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:20.860
- This is gonna sound
strange to some of you,

00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:25.310
but the work I do every day in my job

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:28.840
is work that I feel
relatively comfortable with

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:32.340
and I've been doing it for
the better part of my career.

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:37.340
And I may stress about
issues or stress about

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:41.293
how a particular event or
trip or visit may come out,

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:46.700
but that's very normal,
manageable stress for me.

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:50.220
The most stressed I've ever been in life,

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:53.040
in all honesty, was
when I had to throw out

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:57.823
the opening pitch at the
Nationals game in 2013.

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:02.730
I had just taken my job as
national security advisor

00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:07.730
that month, I am a decent
athlete, but I have

00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:10.840
no particular skill at baseball.

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:15.400
And I was determined to
throw it from the mound

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:19.680
and at least wanted to get
it across in the vicinity

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:22.180
of the plate, to the plate,
at least the distance.

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:27.663
And I was practicing
for weeks, really, and

00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:33.470
I was lucky enough that some of the guys

00:11:33.470 --> 00:11:36.870
on my security detail were
willing to throw with me.

00:11:36.870 --> 00:11:41.870
So we'd go out, we'd throw a
few on a Saturday or Sunday.

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:46.080
And the transition occurred
right as I left my job

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:50.830
at the UN and was coming
over to the White House,

00:11:50.830 --> 00:11:54.000
and my security detail switch
from diplomatic security

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:58.370
that helped me, took care of
me when I was a UN ambassador,

00:11:58.370 --> 00:12:02.480
to Secret Service, and my
lead agent on Secret Service,

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:06.280
who was determined that I was
not gonna humiliate everybody

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:08.620
at the plate.

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:13.620
And so he was kind enough to
throw with me in the tunnel

00:12:14.020 --> 00:12:16.970
right before I went out on the field,

00:12:16.970 --> 00:12:21.810
and I had to go out and I was struck by

00:12:21.810 --> 00:12:24.460
how fast it all happened, you get there

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:26.170
and they say okay, go to the mound.

00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:29.080
And you, 50,000 people are watching you

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:31.930
and you're either gonna get
it or you're not gonna get it.

00:12:31.930 --> 00:12:36.850
And you can imagine that, you
know, I've been the subject

00:12:36.850 --> 00:12:41.493
of a little bit of public
criticism on occasion.

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:47.180
And I could just see Fox
News, you know, doing a loop

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:52.350
on me grounding the ball, you know, so

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:55.220
I got a pair and there was
no time and they said okay,

00:12:55.220 --> 00:12:57.280
Susan Rice, the national security advisor.

00:12:57.280 --> 00:13:00.590
Johnny was there, and
it was mostly cheers,

00:13:00.590 --> 00:13:02.500
but there were some audible boos.

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:04.110
- Yeah, there were, there were.

00:13:04.110 --> 00:13:05.610
- And I just had to throw.

00:13:05.610 --> 00:13:08.580
And I wound up and threw
it and I threw a strike

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:09.930
across the plate.

00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:13.940
And my heart rate went from probably 190

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:18.213
back down to something like 70 and--

00:13:18.213 --> 00:13:19.190
- What would your boss have said--

00:13:19.190 --> 00:13:23.690
- Yeah, exactly, the president
would've ragged on me until,

00:13:23.690 --> 00:13:26.510
you know, there was no end.

00:13:26.510 --> 00:13:27.980
He had done the same, and of course,

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:29.980
managed to throw a pitch, so
he would've talked about me.

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:33.018
And then I was worried, frankly,
on behalf of all womenkind.

00:13:33.018 --> 00:13:34.240
(audience laughter)

00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:36.640
That, you know, if I let the side down,

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:40.130
that it would've been
terrible, so that was stress

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:43.010
of a sort I was not accustomed to

00:13:43.010 --> 00:13:45.931
and I'm never ever doing it again.

00:13:45.931 --> 00:13:46.764
(audience laughter)

00:13:46.764 --> 00:13:49.100
- Not the answer I was
expecting, that's fantastic.

00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:52.137
So let's talk about Syria, let's switch it

00:13:52.137 --> 00:13:54.049
a little bit here, all right.

00:13:54.049 --> 00:13:55.330
(audience laughter)

00:13:55.330 --> 00:13:56.640
- Well, that stresses me out too.

00:13:56.640 --> 00:13:57.920
- All right.

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:01.230
So I know you share with
me like the how, you know,

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:05.440
this is maybe the most
complicated foreign policy issue

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:08.360
that you've had to deal with
intellectually in your career.

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:12.357
In part because, you know,
it seems like there are,

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:15.060
you know, almost nothing but bad options

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:17.330
that cascade into another, you know,

00:14:17.330 --> 00:14:20.970
set of worse options and even
worse scenarios and so forth,

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:23.330
so you know, the first question,

00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:25.640
is there any path to a political solution

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:29.440
and is there any way that
we can mitigate or alleviate

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:31.980
this humanitarian crisis
that's coming out of there?

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:35.240
- Well, honestly, I do
think the crisis in Syria

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:40.240
is the hardest problem to
solve that I've encountered.

00:14:42.760 --> 00:14:45.420
And I felt that way
from the very beginning

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:46.600
four and a half years ago.

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:50.140
And it's only getting
harder, there's no question.

00:14:50.140 --> 00:14:53.410
I do think there is a path
to a political solution

00:14:53.410 --> 00:14:55.690
and we're working very hard towards that

00:14:55.690 --> 00:14:59.270
as we have for quite a
while, but as the dynamics

00:14:59.270 --> 00:15:02.200
have changed and the
circumstances have evolved,

00:15:02.200 --> 00:15:05.360
in some ways, the path is more fraught

00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:10.360
when you consider that this
is now a multi-front conflict.

00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:13.400
It's the opposition against Assad,

00:15:13.400 --> 00:15:16.220
Assad against the
opposition and his people.

00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:20.480
It's ISIL and Al-Nusra,
the Al-Qaeda affiliate,

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:23.020
against both of those sides.

00:15:23.020 --> 00:15:27.240
And it's the world
against ISIL and Al-Nusra.

00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:30.703
So it is a mashup, if
there is such a thing,

00:15:31.790 --> 00:15:33.350
on the battlefield.

00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:35.230
And the only way this gets resolved

00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:37.910
is through a political
solution, there's no other way.

00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:40.970
I mean, you could have local
ceasefires conceivably,

00:15:40.970 --> 00:15:45.260
we have periodic success
in humanitarian access.

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:49.010
But to deal with the
primary concern we have,

00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:53.180
which is defeating ISIL and
ensuring that the threat

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:56.550
that that very pernicious
terrorist organization poses

00:15:56.550 --> 00:16:01.310
is not exacerbated and
doesn't reach our shores

00:16:01.310 --> 00:16:02.970
is our primary focus.

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:05.390
But to accomplish that,
obviously, we also have to deal

00:16:05.390 --> 00:16:07.830
with the fundamental underlying conflict,

00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:10.650
which is a civil conflict, which has taken

00:16:10.650 --> 00:16:12.520
an extraordinary humanitarian toll,

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:13.760
and we obviously have a great interest

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:15.820
in alleviating the suffering.

00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:19.070
And that war can only end
at the negotiating table,

00:16:19.070 --> 00:16:23.150
that's gonna require a
resolution not, frankly,

00:16:23.150 --> 00:16:27.010
dissimilar from what was
envisioned back in Geneva

00:16:27.010 --> 00:16:30.860
in 2012 when we were part of a negotiation

00:16:30.860 --> 00:16:33.340
that established a
framework that envisioned

00:16:33.340 --> 00:16:38.340
a transitional governing
authority, agreed by mutual consent

00:16:38.370 --> 00:16:41.789
between the opposition and the government.

00:16:41.789 --> 00:16:44.110
And the theory of that was, if there had

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:47.480
to be mutual consent, there
couldn't be the perpetuation

00:16:49.210 --> 00:16:50.910
of Assad's leadership in a regime

00:16:50.910 --> 00:16:53.480
that had lost all
legitimacy with its people,

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:55.870
there would have to be other elements

00:16:55.870 --> 00:16:59.470
from the existing government
that had less blood

00:16:59.470 --> 00:17:03.700
on its hands in practice,
elements of the opposition

00:17:03.700 --> 00:17:07.520
who are capable of bringing expertise

00:17:07.520 --> 00:17:09.680
to a transitional governing body,

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:14.530
and then a period of transition,
time to be determined,

00:17:14.530 --> 00:17:17.930
during which a new constitution
could be written, elections

00:17:17.930 --> 00:17:21.560
could be organized, a ceasefire
implemented, et cetera.

00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:23.890
That still remains fundamentally

00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:28.150
the likeliest outcome when eventually

00:17:28.150 --> 00:17:29.870
we get to a political solution,

00:17:29.870 --> 00:17:32.970
we are trying very hard to accelerate

00:17:32.970 --> 00:17:34.800
the achievement of the political solution,

00:17:34.800 --> 00:17:39.800
and Secretary Kerry last
week met in Vienna with

00:17:40.670 --> 00:17:45.130
the foreign ministers of Turkey
and Saudi Arabia and Russia,

00:17:45.130 --> 00:17:47.530
who are key players, obviously, in this.

00:17:47.530 --> 00:17:50.940
And he will meet again, in what I expect

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:53.290
will be just several days'
time, with that group

00:17:53.290 --> 00:17:56.340
and then a broader group
of countries in the region

00:17:56.340 --> 00:17:59.180
with the aim of trying to operationalize

00:17:59.180 --> 00:18:02.203
some kind of political
transition and ceasefire.

00:18:03.330 --> 00:18:07.850
The big thorny issue that remains is when

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:10.510
and whether Assad steps aside.

00:18:10.510 --> 00:18:13.110
Our view remains that he has to go,

00:18:13.110 --> 00:18:15.340
that there can't be an
end to this conflict

00:18:15.340 --> 00:18:20.220
which has taken so many lives
and caused so much suffering

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:23.610
and the, you know, displacement
of over four million people,

00:18:23.610 --> 00:18:28.090
without him departing as
part of the transition.

00:18:28.090 --> 00:18:30.780
But at what stage of the transition

00:18:30.780 --> 00:18:33.560
is something that is being
discussed and debated,

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:36.870
we have very different views
than our coalition partners do

00:18:36.870 --> 00:18:41.340
from the Syrian government,
the Russians and the Iranians.

00:18:41.340 --> 00:18:43.830
But I think there is the potential

00:18:43.830 --> 00:18:47.580
for an arrangement to be agreed

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:51.080
wherein this transition
begins, perhaps with Assad

00:18:51.080 --> 00:18:55.020
still in power, but it
doesn't end with him in power.

00:18:55.020 --> 00:18:57.380
And when, along that
continuum, the transition

00:18:57.380 --> 00:19:01.440
and his removal occurs
is the hardest issue.

00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:04.430
The other challenge is,
frankly, that the opposition

00:19:04.430 --> 00:19:07.530
is extraordinarily fragmented and there is

00:19:07.530 --> 00:19:11.740
no single group or leaders
that speaks for those

00:19:11.740 --> 00:19:13.710
that have been fighting Assad.

00:19:13.710 --> 00:19:18.380
And now the opposition, there
is what we believe to be

00:19:18.380 --> 00:19:21.210
our call and work with
the moderate opposition,

00:19:21.210 --> 00:19:24.060
but they are sometimes co-mingled

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:27.610
with the extremist
opposition, including elements

00:19:27.610 --> 00:19:31.970
like Al-Nusra or those
affiliated with Al-Nusra.

00:19:31.970 --> 00:19:34.150
The only thing that most people,

00:19:34.150 --> 00:19:36.050
I would say almost everybody agrees with,

00:19:36.050 --> 00:19:41.050
is that ISIL is a problem
that can only dealt with

00:19:41.590 --> 00:19:46.150
directly and confrontationally,
and so the aim is

00:19:46.150 --> 00:19:48.860
to get this political
solution as soon as possible

00:19:48.860 --> 00:19:52.580
and turn our collective
efforts without the distraction

00:19:52.580 --> 00:19:56.910
of this ongoing civil
conflict against ISIL.

00:19:56.910 --> 00:19:59.690
And we haven't talked
about Iraq, but obviously,

00:19:59.690 --> 00:20:02.250
that's very much part of the same hole.

00:20:02.250 --> 00:20:05.743
- All right, so it's incredibly
complicated, and if we,

00:20:08.550 --> 00:20:11.990
so for those of us who
are not in there with you

00:20:11.990 --> 00:20:15.700
and the president, Secretary
Kerry and your teams,

00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:18.010
thinking through these issues, you know,

00:20:20.090 --> 00:20:21.630
I've always been curious
sort of, you know,

00:20:21.630 --> 00:20:25.790
what principles, human
principles you kind of pull from

00:20:25.790 --> 00:20:28.080
and where there's, you know,
beyond the issues here,

00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:31.450
is there anything that those of us

00:20:31.450 --> 00:20:35.170
who are not in your world can glean about

00:20:35.170 --> 00:20:37.130
sort of what principles to draw upon

00:20:37.130 --> 00:20:38.610
when you're dealing with a situation

00:20:38.610 --> 00:20:41.190
where there are no good
options and you have to decide

00:20:42.460 --> 00:20:46.203
among options that are, you know, just,

00:20:47.781 --> 00:20:50.080
where you don't have a
positive set of options?

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:52.640
And I'm wondering, you
know, is there anything

00:20:52.640 --> 00:20:54.920
that we can learn from, again,
outside of foreign policy

00:20:54.920 --> 00:20:56.130
in our own lives?

00:20:56.130 --> 00:20:58.870
Any thought you'd have on what
principles that, you know,

00:20:58.870 --> 00:21:00.800
what you've learned, what
principles we can draw upon

00:21:00.800 --> 00:21:03.850
that are relevant to our
lives outside of your work?

00:21:03.850 --> 00:21:06.140
- I'm not sure if the lessons
are directly applicable.

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:09.490
And in the world of foreign
policy and national security,

00:21:09.490 --> 00:21:12.500
at the risk of oversimplifying, you know,

00:21:12.500 --> 00:21:14.900
the first question or the first principle

00:21:14.900 --> 00:21:17.590
that we have to wrestle
with is, quite frankly,

00:21:17.590 --> 00:21:20.750
what is in the United
States national interests?

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:24.290
And that may be different,
the answer to that question,

00:21:24.290 --> 00:21:29.290
very ruthlessly defined,
may be not as edifying

00:21:36.220 --> 00:21:38.940
as we might wanna believe.

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:42.210
It is in the United States'
national interests, for example,

00:21:42.210 --> 00:21:45.940
in Syria, to get deeply
involved in the conflict

00:21:45.940 --> 00:21:48.320
on the ground in a combat role.

00:21:48.320 --> 00:21:50.330
Well, we've seen the consequences

00:21:50.330 --> 00:21:52.130
of the conflict continuing, they are

00:21:54.660 --> 00:21:58.880
deeply insidious consequences
that have horrific outcomes

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:02.980
for the people of Syria,
are destabilizing, giving

00:22:02.980 --> 00:22:05.000
the refugee outflow to
the neighbors, you're now

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:08.003
having implications, with
the refugee flow, for Europe.

00:22:09.110 --> 00:22:10.860
And now we have the challenge of ISIL,

00:22:10.860 --> 00:22:14.567
which does directly threaten
threaten our interests

00:22:14.567 --> 00:22:17.130
and that of our allies and partners.

00:22:17.130 --> 00:22:21.010
But we may have to tailor our solutions

00:22:21.010 --> 00:22:24.790
more narrowly to the
challenge of defeating ISIL

00:22:24.790 --> 00:22:28.990
than to the broader
challenges that are important,

00:22:28.990 --> 00:22:33.990
that we care about, but are not
the ones we must prioritize.

00:22:34.790 --> 00:22:39.240
At the same time, we can
alleviate humanitarian suffering,

00:22:39.240 --> 00:22:42.040
we are the largest contributor
the humanitarian response

00:22:42.040 --> 00:22:44.280
with over 4.5 billion dollars.

00:22:44.280 --> 00:22:49.280
But you know, as human beings,
we all want to do what is,

00:22:52.270 --> 00:22:56.483
you know, the right
thing on a human level.

00:22:58.450 --> 00:23:02.080
And sometimes, I would
argue even oftentimes,

00:23:02.080 --> 00:23:05.240
what is right on a human
level is also what most

00:23:05.240 --> 00:23:09.360
or best serves US interests,
but it's not 100% coincidence,

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:11.760
and sometimes the hardest choices are

00:23:11.760 --> 00:23:15.780
when those things can't
be readily reconciled.

00:23:15.780 --> 00:23:20.100
Now, translating that into,
you know, daily life is hard.

00:23:20.100 --> 00:23:24.390
I mean, when you face circumstances where,

00:23:24.390 --> 00:23:26.700
in your personal and professional life,

00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:31.297
you have no good answers and
no good options, you know,

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:35.850
I think that, I don't know
that I can say, you know,

00:23:35.850 --> 00:23:37.770
take this lesson from the situation room

00:23:37.770 --> 00:23:40.230
and apply it somewhere else.

00:23:40.230 --> 00:23:43.040
But I think, one thing
I'd say is, you know,

00:23:43.040 --> 00:23:46.930
when you examine your options,
you have to ask not only

00:23:46.930 --> 00:23:49.793
what will be the effect
of a particular action,

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:53.460
but you also have to ask what would be

00:23:53.460 --> 00:23:57.820
the effect of inaction and weigh those

00:23:57.820 --> 00:24:02.820
and try to anticipate the
consequences of your actions,

00:24:03.400 --> 00:24:06.123
you know, three or four
steps down the road.

00:24:07.240 --> 00:24:10.560
Because what may be the most immediate,

00:24:10.560 --> 00:24:13.470
least bad answer today could prove

00:24:13.470 --> 00:24:16.540
to be worse down the road.

00:24:16.540 --> 00:24:17.720
- [John] Down the road, yeah.

00:24:17.720 --> 00:24:19.440
- And I suppose vice versa.

00:24:19.440 --> 00:24:24.440
So in trying to play out
almost as if a chess game,

00:24:24.740 --> 00:24:29.050
the actions and reactions, is I guess

00:24:29.050 --> 00:24:31.600
the best analogy I can draw.

00:24:31.600 --> 00:24:32.758
- [John] That's helpful, that's helpful.

00:24:32.758 --> 00:24:37.758
- But it's hard to make
a direct connection.

00:24:38.200 --> 00:24:39.070
- I think that's instructive.

00:24:39.070 --> 00:24:40.880
And so stepping back a little bit, I mean,

00:24:40.880 --> 00:24:43.750
you've been on, you've been a core member

00:24:43.750 --> 00:24:46.490
of the president's foreign
policy team from the beginning,

00:24:46.490 --> 00:24:49.400
right, you started up
at the UN and now down

00:24:49.400 --> 00:24:51.000
at the White House.

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:53.240
And I assume you're gonna
stay till the end, okay,

00:24:53.240 --> 00:24:54.420
I haven't heard otherwise.

00:24:54.420 --> 00:24:56.780
And so I wanna ask you now, you know,

00:24:56.780 --> 00:24:58.830
if you kinda reflect back (mumbles)

00:24:58.830 --> 00:25:00.600
a lot has been accomplished, you know,

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:02.720
what are you most proud of
when you think about a couple,

00:25:02.720 --> 00:25:04.360
you know, if you wanna
mention a few milestones

00:25:04.360 --> 00:25:06.700
or a few accomplishments
that you're most proud of

00:25:06.700 --> 00:25:08.700
that you weren't sure you could get done

00:25:10.718 --> 00:25:13.930
when you joined the team back
actually on the campaign,

00:25:13.930 --> 00:25:17.440
and when the president was sworn in?

00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:19.290
What are you most proud of to date?

00:25:19.290 --> 00:25:21.980
- Well, with the caveat that
that which I'm most proud of

00:25:21.980 --> 00:25:23.620
doesn't mean that I had a direct hand

00:25:23.620 --> 00:25:26.113
in accomplishing all of them.

00:25:27.200 --> 00:25:30.170
And I think vitally important from

00:25:30.170 --> 00:25:33.890
a national security point
of view was the success

00:25:33.890 --> 00:25:36.430
that our economic team had early on

00:25:36.430 --> 00:25:38.170
in turning around their economy.

00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:41.300
Because a huge source of
our international strength

00:25:41.300 --> 00:25:44.410
is the strength of our
economy domestically

00:25:44.410 --> 00:25:49.040
and whether we're able to grow
and produce wealth and jobs,

00:25:49.040 --> 00:25:51.900
and when the president
came into office, we were,

00:25:51.900 --> 00:25:55.420
as you well recall, at the lowest point

00:25:55.420 --> 00:25:57.250
since the Great Depression.

00:25:57.250 --> 00:26:02.250
And now, after years,
we are back to a place

00:26:03.270 --> 00:26:05.677
of economic leadership in the world,

00:26:05.677 --> 00:26:09.570
and the rest of the world
has certainly made progress,

00:26:09.570 --> 00:26:13.010
but you know, the economies
in various different parts

00:26:13.010 --> 00:26:14.630
of the world are still relatively soft,

00:26:14.630 --> 00:26:18.240
we are now not impervious, obviously,

00:26:18.240 --> 00:26:23.240
but the strongest link, and
that has been fundamental

00:26:23.770 --> 00:26:26.620
in restoring our leadership abroad.

00:26:26.620 --> 00:26:28.690
But beyond that, obviously, I mean,

00:26:28.690 --> 00:26:31.900
we've tried to focus on a
number of things, first of all,

00:26:31.900 --> 00:26:36.890
despite the challenges that
still remain in Afghanistan

00:26:36.890 --> 00:26:39.480
and Iraq, when President
Obama came into office,

00:26:39.480 --> 00:26:43.090
we had 180,000 troops in both theaters.

00:26:43.090 --> 00:26:48.010
We are down to less than
10,000 in Afghanistan today.

00:26:48.010 --> 00:26:52.000
We'll be going down to 5500
by the end of next year.

00:26:52.000 --> 00:26:55.080
And we're about 3300 in Iraq.

00:26:55.080 --> 00:26:57.510
And very narrowly tailored missions

00:26:57.510 --> 00:27:00.730
to supporting the Iraqis and the Afghans

00:27:00.730 --> 00:27:02.760
by training and advising
and assisting them,

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:05.863
we're not in combat in either
theater directly anymore.

00:27:06.730 --> 00:27:09.240
And we're also engaged
in counter terrorism

00:27:09.240 --> 00:27:10.760
in a targeted way.

00:27:10.760 --> 00:27:13.280
I think, you know, the
fact that we've been able

00:27:13.280 --> 00:27:18.280
to redeploy and rebalance
and refresh our armed forces

00:27:18.560 --> 00:27:20.580
is vitally important to our readiness

00:27:20.580 --> 00:27:23.240
and our ongoing strength,
even as the nature

00:27:23.240 --> 00:27:25.580
of challenges has evolved.

00:27:25.580 --> 00:27:27.760
But I'm particularly pleased that,

00:27:27.760 --> 00:27:31.580
when you look around the
world, we're rebalancing

00:27:31.580 --> 00:27:33.750
to Asia and the Asia Pacific.

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:35.470
A great deal of our time and energy

00:27:35.470 --> 00:27:37.950
that doesn't get a lot
of attention in the press

00:27:37.950 --> 00:27:42.950
is devoted to the work that
we're doing in the Asia Pacific.

00:27:43.390 --> 00:27:46.310
We have just had, over
the last several months,

00:27:46.310 --> 00:27:48.460
high profile and very impactful visits

00:27:48.460 --> 00:27:52.350
from the Japanese leader,
the Chinese leader,

00:27:52.350 --> 00:27:54.720
the South Korean leader just this week,

00:27:54.720 --> 00:27:56.003
the Indonesian leader.

00:27:57.340 --> 00:28:00.020
We are heading to Asia next month, again,

00:28:00.020 --> 00:28:04.060
we have gone, if not every
year, then twice every year,

00:28:04.060 --> 00:28:08.190
in the recent past, for very important

00:28:08.190 --> 00:28:09.960
summit meetings out there.

00:28:09.960 --> 00:28:12.530
And we've just been able to conclude

00:28:12.530 --> 00:28:15.380
the Trans-Pacific
Partnership trade agreement,

00:28:15.380 --> 00:28:19.620
which is a signature element
of the Asia rebalance,

00:28:19.620 --> 00:28:22.320
so I think that's something that will be

00:28:22.320 --> 00:28:24.250
of lasting significance.

00:28:24.250 --> 00:28:29.250
We've all worked very hard on
the issue of non-proliferation

00:28:29.460 --> 00:28:33.990
and trying to reduce
the threat from loose,

00:28:33.990 --> 00:28:35.560
what some people call loose nukes,

00:28:35.560 --> 00:28:39.040
which is not the term we're
supposed to use, but you know,

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:41.070
we have strengthened nuclear security

00:28:41.070 --> 00:28:43.630
in a very meaningful
way, and then, of course,

00:28:43.630 --> 00:28:46.940
we've been able to reach the agreement to

00:28:46.940 --> 00:28:50.020
prevent Iran from
obtaining a nuclear weapon

00:28:50.020 --> 00:28:54.400
with the support of the
international community.

00:28:54.400 --> 00:28:58.550
And I think that will
prove, if fully implemented,

00:28:58.550 --> 00:29:03.550
to have been an unexpected
and yet very meaningful means

00:29:05.920 --> 00:29:09.580
of constraining a very
dangerous nuclear program

00:29:09.580 --> 00:29:12.390
and ensuring that Iran is not able

00:29:12.390 --> 00:29:15.240
to obtain a nuclear weapon.

00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:19.250
So that's another thing, I think frankly,

00:29:19.250 --> 00:29:21.690
when we look back on our time,

00:29:21.690 --> 00:29:24.240
I certainly will be very pleased

00:29:24.240 --> 00:29:27.170
that we've made an opening with Cuba

00:29:27.170 --> 00:29:32.170
and that 50 years of failed
policy have been supplanted

00:29:33.730 --> 00:29:36.140
by what I think will
prove over the long term

00:29:36.140 --> 00:29:39.320
to be the most effective
means of opening up Cuba,

00:29:39.320 --> 00:29:41.480
both politically and economically.

00:29:41.480 --> 00:29:46.420
And finally, and I could
go on, but you know,

00:29:46.420 --> 00:29:51.420
as a policy maker, but also
as a mother and as a citizen,

00:29:53.320 --> 00:29:56.330
you know, one of the issues
that I'm most concerned about,

00:29:56.330 --> 00:29:59.430
and I know the president has
put the highest priority on,

00:29:59.430 --> 00:30:02.960
is climate change and trying
to address that threat.

00:30:02.960 --> 00:30:07.060
And we are now heading
into Paris in December

00:30:07.060 --> 00:30:12.060
for the COP meeting on climate change

00:30:12.190 --> 00:30:16.330
with the real potential
of achieving an agreement

00:30:16.330 --> 00:30:20.530
that will ratchet down
emissions in a meaningful way,

00:30:20.530 --> 00:30:25.520
and we've been able to negotiate
with China, the largest,

00:30:25.520 --> 00:30:28.770
we're the first and the
second largest emitters

00:30:28.770 --> 00:30:33.770
of greenhouse gases, an
unprecedented mutual commitment

00:30:34.860 --> 00:30:38.860
to reduce our carbon output.

00:30:38.860 --> 00:30:41.830
And that has set the
stage for us to be able

00:30:41.830 --> 00:30:44.950
to gain commitments from countries

00:30:44.950 --> 00:30:49.950
amounting to 80%, 85% now of
emissions around the world

00:30:50.210 --> 00:30:53.870
to make very concrete national commitments

00:30:53.870 --> 00:30:58.180
to reduce their emission, so
if we can get this agreement

00:30:58.180 --> 00:31:03.180
in Paris and if it is
meaningful and substantive,

00:31:03.960 --> 00:31:06.450
that will be a huge step, and the steps

00:31:06.450 --> 00:31:08.270
we've taken domestically to reinforce that

00:31:08.270 --> 00:31:10.900
are a critical part as well.

00:31:10.900 --> 00:31:13.060
- Got it, so if I could pivot a little bit

00:31:13.060 --> 00:31:16.930
just to an area that you
don't get to talk too much,

00:31:16.930 --> 00:31:20.810
at least so much about
publicly, and I think, you know,

00:31:20.810 --> 00:31:22.290
but I know you well enough to know

00:31:22.290 --> 00:31:23.750
that you are passionate about some things

00:31:23.750 --> 00:31:25.390
that go beyond foreign policy.

00:31:25.390 --> 00:31:26.223
- [Susan] You think?

00:31:26.223 --> 00:31:27.056
- Right.

00:31:27.056 --> 00:31:28.490
And they're things that are
important to our nation,

00:31:28.490 --> 00:31:30.890
and one of the things that
you share with me, Susan,

00:31:30.890 --> 00:31:35.080
you know, is your passion for
sort of the improving equality

00:31:36.300 --> 00:31:40.170
of opportunity in this
nation and globally.

00:31:40.170 --> 00:31:44.437
And so when you think about
hot button issues that are like

00:31:47.020 --> 00:31:50.170
criminal justice reform and
policing and immigration

00:31:50.170 --> 00:31:52.580
and healthcare, K through
12 education, gay marriage,

00:31:52.580 --> 00:31:55.910
I mean, so many of them
relate to this, you know,

00:31:55.910 --> 00:32:00.530
are related to the issue
of equality of opportunity.

00:32:00.530 --> 00:32:03.500
And some of these issues,
you all really moved

00:32:03.500 --> 00:32:04.710
the needle on it, within the context

00:32:04.710 --> 00:32:07.070
of the administration,
over the last seven years.

00:32:07.070 --> 00:32:09.480
Some are rising to the top now.

00:32:09.480 --> 00:32:12.080
But I just, I wanna get your, you know,

00:32:12.080 --> 00:32:14.450
your point of view, not
necessarily from the point of the,

00:32:14.450 --> 00:32:16.250
you know, the perspective
of the administration, okay,

00:32:16.250 --> 00:32:17.500
'cause this is not your lane--

00:32:17.500 --> 00:32:19.730
- [Susan] I would not get out of my lane.

00:32:19.730 --> 00:32:21.280
- But given that you, you know, we're here

00:32:21.280 --> 00:32:23.010
with members of the New Haven community

00:32:23.010 --> 00:32:25.510
and students and professors
here at the university

00:32:26.440 --> 00:32:28.900
who are ambitious and
idealistic and are tackling

00:32:28.900 --> 00:32:31.070
some of these tough issues,
you know, in your mind,

00:32:31.070 --> 00:32:35.270
you know, what are the
one or two critical levers

00:32:35.270 --> 00:32:38.460
that we should focus on in our nation

00:32:38.460 --> 00:32:41.093
to move the needle on
equality of opportunity?

00:32:43.282 --> 00:32:44.829
- [Female Speaker] Excuse
me, madam secretary,

00:32:44.829 --> 00:32:48.130
I'm taking a small call back
here and we can't hear you.

00:32:48.130 --> 00:32:48.963
- [John] Okay.

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:51.395
How do we...

00:32:51.395 --> 00:32:54.470
(background noise drowns
out female speaker)

00:32:54.470 --> 00:32:55.303
Okay.

00:32:55.303 --> 00:32:56.136
- I'm sorry.

00:32:56.136 --> 00:32:57.430
- [John] Can't hear it.

00:32:57.430 --> 00:32:58.758
- Can you hear better now?

00:32:58.758 --> 00:33:00.080
- [Female Speaker] Yes.

00:33:00.080 --> 00:33:03.380
- Thank you for saying it,
I wish you'd said it sooner.

00:33:03.380 --> 00:33:04.213
Thank you.

00:33:04.213 --> 00:33:05.046
- [John] Thank you.

00:33:06.560 --> 00:33:10.002
- These are unusual contraptions.

00:33:10.002 --> 00:33:11.900
(audience laughter)

00:33:11.900 --> 00:33:12.910
- [John] Can you hear me?

00:33:12.910 --> 00:33:14.600
Yeah, okay, sorry.

00:33:14.600 --> 00:33:15.960
- So repeat the question.

00:33:15.960 --> 00:33:20.960
- So the question is, when
you think about, you know,

00:33:21.400 --> 00:33:24.810
addressing, you know, inequality here,

00:33:24.810 --> 00:33:26.550
inequality of opportunity in this nation,

00:33:26.550 --> 00:33:28.380
and you can speak globally if you'd like.

00:33:28.380 --> 00:33:32.310
You know, if you, you know, if
you were, folks in the crowd,

00:33:32.310 --> 00:33:34.530
if you, you know, what should we,

00:33:34.530 --> 00:33:35.880
what can we move the needle on,

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:37.180
what are the things, the levers,

00:33:37.180 --> 00:33:42.151
that we should be focusing
on to build a nation

00:33:42.151 --> 00:33:47.151
that really addresses and moves the needle

00:33:47.810 --> 00:33:50.050
on equality of opportunity?

00:33:50.050 --> 00:33:51.520
- Well, I mean, Johnny, I think you know,

00:33:51.520 --> 00:33:54.080
this is something I cared deeply about,

00:33:54.080 --> 00:33:56.323
but it's not an area that I'm expert in.

00:33:58.660 --> 00:34:03.300
I think, you heard from the
introduction of my background,

00:34:03.300 --> 00:34:06.850
and Johnny's, I call
him Johnny, by the way.

00:34:06.850 --> 00:34:08.233
Some people call him John.

00:34:09.840 --> 00:34:13.743
That we've taken very
different career paths.

00:34:14.690 --> 00:34:17.910
But we grew up in the same
household with the same values

00:34:17.910 --> 00:34:21.320
and the same parents and the same,

00:34:21.320 --> 00:34:24.740
many of the same, I think,
motivations and concerns.

00:34:24.740 --> 00:34:29.030
And I think this is an
area that has united us,

00:34:29.030 --> 00:34:32.710
John's work is really
about building ladders

00:34:32.710 --> 00:34:37.710
for those who have not had necessarily

00:34:37.950 --> 00:34:42.690
all of the privilege and
opportunity that has been,

00:34:42.690 --> 00:34:45.783
that we've been blessed with
and that many are blessed with,

00:34:46.880 --> 00:34:50.120
so that they have the
mentorship and the preparation

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:55.120
and the training to go
into high powered careers

00:34:56.610 --> 00:34:58.990
and opportunities in
business or management

00:34:58.990 --> 00:35:03.650
or the non-profit world, but
the theory of John's non-profit

00:35:04.840 --> 00:35:08.120
is that, if you catch
a young person early on

00:35:09.110 --> 00:35:14.110
and give him or her, with
all of their inherent talent,

00:35:15.120 --> 00:35:17.713
the opportunities, the
mentorship, the training,

00:35:18.550 --> 00:35:21.420
the support to succeed,
then the likelihood

00:35:21.420 --> 00:35:23.410
of their success goes way up.

00:35:23.410 --> 00:35:28.357
And my own personal passion
that, as Johnny said,

00:35:30.240 --> 00:35:32.670
is not the focus of my workday today,

00:35:32.670 --> 00:35:37.050
but it is fundamentally
an animating motivation,

00:35:37.050 --> 00:35:40.180
is I believe deeply, as many of us do,

00:35:40.180 --> 00:35:43.860
in the fundamental worth and
equality of every human being.

00:35:43.860 --> 00:35:46.480
And I believe fundamentally,
and this may be part

00:35:46.480 --> 00:35:49.410
of my experience as an
African American woman,

00:35:49.410 --> 00:35:52.830
that there is so much
more talent out there

00:35:52.830 --> 00:35:57.830
than ever is able to rise to the surface,

00:35:57.960 --> 00:36:02.960
and that we have not only an
obligation and an interest

00:36:03.090 --> 00:36:06.710
in nurturing and cultivating
that talent, but that,

00:36:06.710 --> 00:36:09.920
as a matter of national
strength and even, indeed,

00:36:09.920 --> 00:36:12.700
as a matter of national
security, to the extent

00:36:12.700 --> 00:36:15.860
that we are not cultivating
and nurturing that talent,

00:36:15.860 --> 00:36:19.880
wherever it may reside, we are diminished,

00:36:19.880 --> 00:36:22.380
we are not firing on all cylinders,

00:36:22.380 --> 00:36:25.423
we're competing with one
hand tied behind our back.

00:36:26.280 --> 00:36:30.630
And so I believe passionately
that we can do better

00:36:30.630 --> 00:36:32.560
and we have to do better.

00:36:32.560 --> 00:36:35.980
And that, I think, frankly boils down to

00:36:35.980 --> 00:36:40.740
what you all would assume
and that is sort of obvious,

00:36:40.740 --> 00:36:42.510
which is education.

00:36:42.510 --> 00:36:47.510
And starting at the earliest
stages, at, you know,

00:36:47.885 --> 00:36:51.150
early education, preschool,
and all the way up

00:36:51.150 --> 00:36:56.150
through higher education, being
able to provide young people

00:36:58.300 --> 00:37:02.670
with the support that
they need and deserve,

00:37:02.670 --> 00:37:05.590
and that means that education
has to be affordable,

00:37:05.590 --> 00:37:09.700
it has to be of a decent
quality, it has to be safe

00:37:09.700 --> 00:37:14.500
and it has to be available
to kids whether they come

00:37:14.500 --> 00:37:18.310
from inner city New
Haven or from, you know,

00:37:18.310 --> 00:37:21.980
the wealthiest suburbs elsewhere.

00:37:21.980 --> 00:37:26.980
And frankly, we still have
not done, in my judgment,

00:37:28.210 --> 00:37:33.210
as a nation, a sufficient job
of focusing on that deficit

00:37:33.780 --> 00:37:37.660
and investing in the talent
that this nation has,

00:37:37.660 --> 00:37:42.280
and we are one of the most skilled,

00:37:42.280 --> 00:37:47.023
diverse, rich societies in the world.

00:37:47.900 --> 00:37:51.230
And when I look out internationally
and I think, you know,

00:37:51.230 --> 00:37:54.830
how do we sustain American leadership

00:37:54.830 --> 00:37:59.240
through the 21st century and
beyond, how do we maximize

00:37:59.240 --> 00:38:02.430
our inherent strength, and I don't mean

00:38:02.430 --> 00:38:06.000
just our military strength or
even our economic strength,

00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:10.540
but our social and our
intellectual strength

00:38:10.540 --> 00:38:12.320
and our cultural strength.

00:38:12.320 --> 00:38:15.190
It necessitates that we take advantage of

00:38:15.190 --> 00:38:19.690
this extraordinary fabric
that is our talented

00:38:19.690 --> 00:38:20.960
and diverse society, and if we

00:38:20.960 --> 00:38:24.410
are not doing that adequately, not only

00:38:24.410 --> 00:38:28.840
are we leaving behind so many,
but I really truly believe

00:38:28.840 --> 00:38:33.570
we are not doing our, we're not fulfilling

00:38:33.570 --> 00:38:35.110
our potential as a global leader.

00:38:35.110 --> 00:38:36.290
- Great, great.

00:38:36.290 --> 00:38:39.120
So getting more personal, okay.

00:38:39.120 --> 00:38:41.040
- We haven't gotten personal yet.

00:38:41.040 --> 00:38:42.020
You and I, we can get real personal.

00:38:42.020 --> 00:38:45.870
- All right, so you're
a mom of two, right,

00:38:45.870 --> 00:38:49.420
you have a seventh grader
and you have a son,

00:38:49.420 --> 00:38:51.900
seventh grade daughter,
Maris, son in high school

00:38:51.900 --> 00:38:54.705
who's actually accompanied you here, Jake.

00:38:54.705 --> 00:38:59.705
And you're a wife of 23
years, is that right?

00:39:00.092 --> 00:39:01.970
How many, how many years, okay.

00:39:01.970 --> 00:39:04.087
And you spend a lot of time, I mean,

00:39:04.087 --> 00:39:07.600
and I've been so impressed
by how you've been able

00:39:07.600 --> 00:39:09.580
to spend so much time over the course

00:39:09.580 --> 00:39:13.080
of the last eight years
caring for our father,

00:39:13.080 --> 00:39:18.080
our mom now, you know, and
finding time to do that.

00:39:18.209 --> 00:39:22.260
So this is not a question
about work-life balance,

00:39:22.260 --> 00:39:26.140
'cause I know that's not,
you know, even a, you know,

00:39:26.140 --> 00:39:27.840
part of a vocabulary here.

00:39:27.840 --> 00:39:32.840
But I wanted to see if
you have any, you know,

00:39:34.500 --> 00:39:37.840
guiding principles around
that you could share

00:39:37.840 --> 00:39:42.120
with the audience here about
how to stay above water

00:39:42.120 --> 00:39:45.320
and not let the most important balls drop.

00:39:45.320 --> 00:39:47.543
How do you approach it,
how do you, knowing that

00:39:47.543 --> 00:39:50.050
it's not perfect, knowing
that you've gotta make

00:39:50.050 --> 00:39:52.223
tough trade offs in your personal life.

00:39:53.583 --> 00:39:55.050
You've kept the most important,

00:39:55.050 --> 00:39:57.700
I can attest to that
personally, I really admire

00:39:57.700 --> 00:40:00.090
how you figured out how to do that.

00:40:00.090 --> 00:40:02.240
And it comes with trade
offs, but any, you know,

00:40:02.240 --> 00:40:04.120
what advice would you
have for the folks here,

00:40:04.120 --> 00:40:07.550
whether they're undergrad,
grad students here

00:40:07.550 --> 00:40:12.410
or folks who are not
students here, on how to keep

00:40:12.410 --> 00:40:14.780
the most important balls
from dropping when you're--

00:40:14.780 --> 00:40:17.240
- Well, let me, first
of all, I'm not trying

00:40:17.240 --> 00:40:20.480
to blow smoke here,
but Johnny is giving me

00:40:20.480 --> 00:40:25.420
a lot of credit, and the
fact is, we have, well,

00:40:25.420 --> 00:40:27.370
we've been blessed with
two wonderful parents,

00:40:27.370 --> 00:40:30.510
our father passed away
four and a half years ago,

00:40:30.510 --> 00:40:33.280
our mom is still alive, she,
I think, would've enjoyed

00:40:34.220 --> 00:40:36.950
being here today, she's in Washington

00:40:36.950 --> 00:40:40.860
and her health is fragile.

00:40:40.860 --> 00:40:45.860
And we have, together, I
think, done a pretty good job

00:40:45.960 --> 00:40:48.053
of trying to support our parents.

00:40:49.130 --> 00:40:52.550
And we've succeeded because
we've done it together,

00:40:52.550 --> 00:40:57.550
along with my husband and
Johnny's wife and our kids.

00:40:57.720 --> 00:41:01.290
And the answer to your
question is because, you know,

00:41:01.290 --> 00:41:06.290
this is teamwork, no one
individual can manage, you know,

00:41:08.560 --> 00:41:12.070
the challenges of taking care of kids,

00:41:12.070 --> 00:41:14.300
taking care of family,
taking care of yourself.

00:41:14.300 --> 00:41:17.260
It does require partners.

00:41:17.260 --> 00:41:19.330
- [John] So marry well, that's
your advice, marry well?

00:41:19.330 --> 00:41:20.780
- Definitely marry well.

00:41:22.470 --> 00:41:26.770
Yeah, I've been extraordinarily
blessed to have this,

00:41:26.770 --> 00:41:31.300
the most wonderful,
supportive and, you know,

00:41:31.300 --> 00:41:34.853
talented husband, you have,
you've done very well yourself.

00:41:35.702 --> 00:41:37.354
(audience laughter)

00:41:37.354 --> 00:41:39.250
And fortunately, everybody
likes each other,

00:41:39.250 --> 00:41:40.940
so we really, we have been a team.

00:41:40.940 --> 00:41:44.800
But if I had some advice,
I'd say the following.

00:41:44.800 --> 00:41:48.170
First of all, everybody is
different, every circumstance

00:41:48.170 --> 00:41:51.060
is different, and I hate
these conversations where,

00:41:51.060 --> 00:41:54.147
you know, women debate,
where they lean in, lean out,

00:41:54.147 --> 00:41:57.740
you know, tell everybody
how to handle their lives.

00:41:57.740 --> 00:42:01.640
My view on this is
everybody's life is different,

00:42:01.640 --> 00:42:04.020
everybody's circumstance is
different, people really need

00:42:04.020 --> 00:42:05.840
to stop telling each other what to do

00:42:05.840 --> 00:42:07.960
and just support each other and recognize

00:42:07.960 --> 00:42:11.280
that everybody is trying to
make the best choices they can

00:42:11.280 --> 00:42:12.940
in their particular circumstances

00:42:12.940 --> 00:42:15.850
and there's no one size fits all formula.

00:42:15.850 --> 00:42:17.970
The only thing I can say
from my vantage point

00:42:17.970 --> 00:42:21.690
that has been a valuable principle for me

00:42:21.690 --> 00:42:24.370
is that family comes first.

00:42:24.370 --> 00:42:29.370
And even in a job like mine,
family comes first, because,

00:42:29.480 --> 00:42:31.100
and your dearest friends, because

00:42:31.100 --> 00:42:33.860
when everything else falls apart,

00:42:33.860 --> 00:42:36.740
that's what you've got left,
you've got the people you love

00:42:36.740 --> 00:42:38.750
and the people who love you.

00:42:38.750 --> 00:42:42.680
And if you ever forget that, then I think

00:42:42.680 --> 00:42:45.610
you're missing something fundamental.

00:42:45.610 --> 00:42:48.780
And I just wanna say, I
mean, I've thought a lot

00:42:48.780 --> 00:42:52.020
about this in my own life
and I've just seen it modeled

00:42:52.020 --> 00:42:56.530
in the most extraordinary
way, having had the privilege

00:42:56.530 --> 00:42:59.550
of working very closely
with Vice President Biden

00:42:59.550 --> 00:43:03.573
who has just gone through
extraordinary loss.

00:43:04.660 --> 00:43:08.490
And in the context of working, you know,

00:43:08.490 --> 00:43:10.710
the second toughest job in the world.

00:43:10.710 --> 00:43:13.840
And he has been there for his family,

00:43:13.840 --> 00:43:15.500
his family has been there for him,

00:43:15.500 --> 00:43:17.880
he was there for Beau,
just as he was there

00:43:17.880 --> 00:43:21.000
for Beau and Hunter early in their lives

00:43:21.000 --> 00:43:23.927
when they suffered extraordinary loss.

00:43:23.927 --> 00:43:28.927
And he is living proof that
you can and you must invest

00:43:31.100 --> 00:43:34.290
your utmost in your
family, even as you take on

00:43:34.290 --> 00:43:37.750
the most challenging
responsibilities that you might find,

00:43:37.750 --> 00:43:39.600
whether in government
or the private sector

00:43:39.600 --> 00:43:41.930
or the non-profit world.

00:43:41.930 --> 00:43:46.630
So you know, there have been
times in my professional life

00:43:46.630 --> 00:43:49.130
and personal life where it's, you know,

00:43:49.130 --> 00:43:52.280
we've hit very painful patches.

00:43:52.280 --> 00:43:56.030
But what has made it all
sustainable and worthwhile

00:43:56.030 --> 00:43:59.450
is that we've had each other and we've had

00:44:00.380 --> 00:44:03.400
our extended family and
the love and support

00:44:03.400 --> 00:44:07.433
of our kids and our
parents and our spouses.

00:44:08.480 --> 00:44:12.520
And I think, you know,
it's a simple truism,

00:44:12.520 --> 00:44:17.520
but I think sometimes,
in the rush and the press

00:44:17.550 --> 00:44:20.560
of professional life and stress,

00:44:20.560 --> 00:44:23.443
that can sometimes be
forgotten, and it shouldn't be.

00:44:24.430 --> 00:44:26.650
- All right, so before we
open it up for questions,

00:44:26.650 --> 00:44:29.160
I have one last one and it
builds upon what you said,

00:44:29.160 --> 00:44:34.160
and it speaks to, you know, you've had a,

00:44:36.910 --> 00:44:39.240
quite a rollercoaster ride, right,

00:44:39.240 --> 00:44:41.370
over the course of the last few years.

00:44:41.370 --> 00:44:46.280
I mean, you had a great run at the UN,

00:44:46.280 --> 00:44:48.153
felt like you got a lot done there.

00:44:49.140 --> 00:44:52.513
Benghazi came up and that got politicized.

00:44:53.640 --> 00:44:56.454
The whole secretary of state stuff.

00:44:56.454 --> 00:44:59.270
And now what you talked
about, some of the things

00:44:59.270 --> 00:45:02.060
that you've really been excited about

00:45:02.060 --> 00:45:04.560
over the course of your tenure here

00:45:04.560 --> 00:45:06.940
as a national security advisor.

00:45:06.940 --> 00:45:10.180
So there are probably
lots of people here today

00:45:10.180 --> 00:45:14.410
whose parents are worrying how

00:45:14.410 --> 00:45:17.640
they will handle adversity
when it comes up.

00:45:17.640 --> 00:45:19.320
How do, you know, and handle setbacks,

00:45:19.320 --> 00:45:21.840
because so many folks here up at Yale

00:45:22.700 --> 00:45:26.270
haven't had big setbacks yet.

00:45:26.270 --> 00:45:30.120
And these are, this
could be a new frontier

00:45:30.120 --> 00:45:32.010
because you've had so much
success in your lives to date,

00:45:32.010 --> 00:45:36.480
and I'm wondering, you know,
what advice would you have

00:45:36.480 --> 00:45:39.310
for these folks about what you draw on

00:45:39.310 --> 00:45:43.200
when you have setbacks, when
you have adversity, you know,

00:45:43.200 --> 00:45:45.160
when that comes, where do you draw on?

00:45:45.160 --> 00:45:46.470
Do you draw from family?

00:45:46.470 --> 00:45:51.300
You know, what do you do,
even when, especially in times

00:45:51.300 --> 00:45:54.210
where you feel like, you know,

00:45:54.210 --> 00:45:55.590
you may or may not have done anything

00:45:55.590 --> 00:45:57.390
to bring that adversity on yourself.

00:45:59.060 --> 00:46:01.190
And that's a tough question,
it's a personal question,

00:46:01.190 --> 00:46:02.820
but anything you would
share with the group

00:46:02.820 --> 00:46:05.473
about how to deal with
adversity when it comes?

00:46:06.400 --> 00:46:09.780
- Well, I think the first
answer is the answer

00:46:09.780 --> 00:46:14.780
I already gave, which is
that, family and friends

00:46:15.720 --> 00:46:19.250
and the strength that they provide.

00:46:19.250 --> 00:46:24.250
But I think also, the strength
has gotta come from inside.

00:46:25.640 --> 00:46:30.640
And now just speaking very
very personally, I have,

00:46:33.250 --> 00:46:37.000
for better or for worse,
I've always known who I am,

00:46:37.000 --> 00:46:39.280
I've always known what I believe,

00:46:39.280 --> 00:46:44.250
I've always tried to do what
I do with the utmost integrity

00:46:44.250 --> 00:46:49.250
and intensity and I
believe that, you know,

00:46:50.690 --> 00:46:54.330
I know who I am and I
also know who I'm not.

00:46:54.330 --> 00:46:59.330
And when I'm watching a
caricature or a critique of myself

00:47:03.030 --> 00:47:08.030
that is painful and
personalized, I'm able to,

00:47:11.920 --> 00:47:14.513
I don't wanna suggest
that I don't feel the,

00:47:15.400 --> 00:47:17.650
I don't feel offended or I don't feel hurt

00:47:17.650 --> 00:47:22.650
in some instances by it, but
I'm also able to distinguish

00:47:23.670 --> 00:47:26.250
between what I know about myself

00:47:26.250 --> 00:47:31.250
and who I know myself to
be and what is being said.

00:47:31.340 --> 00:47:36.007
And there, to the extent
that I'm able to understand

00:47:39.100 --> 00:47:41.930
and maintain that distinction,

00:47:41.930 --> 00:47:45.150
I can keep a certain distance from it.

00:47:45.150 --> 00:47:46.717
What's harder, and I'll tell you that,

00:47:46.717 --> 00:47:50.433
and you know this, but I'll
just acknowledge it here.

00:47:51.820 --> 00:47:54.570
The hardest thing for me,
going through the period

00:47:54.570 --> 00:47:59.570
you described in 2012, was
not what it felt like for me.

00:48:03.070 --> 00:48:05.640
The hardest thing was what I saw it do

00:48:05.640 --> 00:48:10.250
to my mom and to my daughter who were

00:48:10.250 --> 00:48:12.360
in very different ways,
in different, I mean,

00:48:12.360 --> 00:48:14.130
what was, Maris was--

00:48:14.130 --> 00:48:16.810
- [John] Fifth grade, yeah, 11 to 12?

00:48:16.810 --> 00:48:18.227
- No, she's 12 now.

00:48:18.227 --> 00:48:20.380
(laughs)

00:48:20.380 --> 00:48:21.430
She was nine.

00:48:24.560 --> 00:48:26.350
It really hit her very very hard.

00:48:26.350 --> 00:48:29.790
She was old enough to
be able to understand

00:48:29.790 --> 00:48:33.140
that her mom is being
vilified on the television

00:48:34.090 --> 00:48:36.657
and hear the things that were being said

00:48:38.280 --> 00:48:41.770
and sort of understand
why it was happening,

00:48:41.770 --> 00:48:46.770
but not really understand
that it, what you see

00:48:48.380 --> 00:48:52.080
isn't necessarily all that
there is to the story.

00:48:52.080 --> 00:48:57.080
And it was, it really was briefly

00:48:57.110 --> 00:49:00.380
but intensely destabilizing for her.

00:49:00.380 --> 00:49:05.380
And then my mom, who's tough as nails

00:49:06.780 --> 00:49:09.723
but has an addiction to cable news,

00:49:13.140 --> 00:49:14.750
just couldn't turn the television off,

00:49:14.750 --> 00:49:17.810
but also, it was driving
her absolutely insane.

00:49:17.810 --> 00:49:19.970
And this after she'd just
come out of the hospital

00:49:19.970 --> 00:49:22.320
and been through, you know, surgery

00:49:22.320 --> 00:49:25.170
and very difficult recovery
and all this stuff.

00:49:25.170 --> 00:49:30.170
And you know, she just was beside herself.

00:49:30.700 --> 00:49:33.480
And it was the only
time that I was really,

00:49:33.480 --> 00:49:36.620
I hate to say this, but I
was glad dad wasn't alive.

00:49:36.620 --> 00:49:39.980
'Cause if dad had been alive,
he would've taken his shotgun

00:49:41.800 --> 00:49:44.883
up to Capitol Hill and it
would've been all over.

00:49:46.210 --> 00:49:48.907
- [John] Grew up in South
Carolina, all right, (mumbles).

00:49:52.340 --> 00:49:56.670
- You know, you just, the
other thing I'd say is

00:49:56.670 --> 00:49:59.790
that you keep working and you
keep doing what you believe.

00:49:59.790 --> 00:50:04.790
And in some ways, it was great
to be at the UN at the time,

00:50:06.530 --> 00:50:09.790
where the issues that we were working on

00:50:09.790 --> 00:50:12.290
and that my fellow ambassadors cared about

00:50:12.290 --> 00:50:16.580
were far removed from
the noise in Washington.

00:50:16.580 --> 00:50:21.440
It never, it was incredible
to me that, as UN ambassador,

00:50:21.440 --> 00:50:25.070
I would have to go
before the UN press corps

00:50:25.070 --> 00:50:28.577
multiple times a week and make
statements or take questions,

00:50:28.577 --> 00:50:32.280
and never once, at the UN,
got asked about Benghazi

00:50:33.440 --> 00:50:35.030
because they didn't care, it was,

00:50:35.030 --> 00:50:38.940
they saw it as a
manufactured political story

00:50:38.940 --> 00:50:43.370
and they wanted to ask about
the issues on the UN agenda.

00:50:43.370 --> 00:50:45.662
Come to Washington, it's
a little bit different.

00:50:45.662 --> 00:50:47.050
- A little different, yeah, got it.

00:50:47.050 --> 00:50:48.253
Okay, let's open it up.

00:50:53.860 --> 00:50:55.380
Yeah, why don't you come on up,

00:50:55.380 --> 00:51:00.280
as you will be a much more
capable moderator than I.

00:51:02.150 --> 00:51:03.920
So how do wanna run this?

00:51:03.920 --> 00:51:04.753
- She's running it.

00:51:04.753 --> 00:51:07.340
- So first, I just wanna make
sure we give a large round

00:51:07.340 --> 00:51:10.439
of applause to Ambassador
Rice and John Rice.

00:51:10.439 --> 00:51:12.689
(applause)

00:51:21.774 --> 00:51:23.630
- And I wanna thank
Johnny, but encourage him

00:51:23.630 --> 00:51:24.952
to keep his day job.

00:51:24.952 --> 00:51:27.895
(audience laughter)

00:51:27.895 --> 00:51:29.820
You did a great job.

00:51:29.820 --> 00:51:33.520
- So I am the moderator
of this Q&amp;A session,

00:51:33.520 --> 00:51:37.330
and I may need to moderate
up here as well, as I see.

00:51:37.330 --> 00:51:40.070
Oh, there was a friendly
handshake that just happened.

00:51:40.070 --> 00:51:42.860
I am the master of Timothy Dwight College,

00:51:42.860 --> 00:51:45.770
I'm Mary Lui and it's
a real pleasure for me

00:51:45.770 --> 00:51:49.010
to greet all of you and certainly welcome

00:51:49.010 --> 00:51:52.480
our two wonderful guests who
shared with us their remarks

00:51:53.340 --> 00:51:56.230
and they covered so much
ground in their fireside chat,

00:51:56.230 --> 00:51:59.010
so I think there's gonna
be numerous opportunities

00:51:59.010 --> 00:52:01.480
to ask questions to follow up.

00:52:01.480 --> 00:52:03.100
I also wanna take the time really quickly

00:52:03.100 --> 00:52:06.260
to thank President Salovey
for his introductory remarks,

00:52:06.260 --> 00:52:07.590
thank you for that.

00:52:07.590 --> 00:52:11.270
So we are gonna open up
for questions right now,

00:52:11.270 --> 00:52:13.160
so please go ahead and line up,

00:52:13.160 --> 00:52:15.570
either two microphones right here,

00:52:15.570 --> 00:52:18.673
and just be in a very orderly fashion.

00:52:20.120 --> 00:52:23.500
And we'll alternate between
the two microphones.

00:52:23.500 --> 00:52:25.730
I'm seeing two very large lines forming,

00:52:25.730 --> 00:52:28.950
and we will not able to get
through all of the questions,

00:52:28.950 --> 00:52:31.970
I already am apologizing
to the people in the back.

00:52:31.970 --> 00:52:34.350
So I will just say, once
you ask your question,

00:52:34.350 --> 00:52:36.110
please step aside from the microphone

00:52:36.110 --> 00:52:41.110
and let's let our
wonderful guests respond.

00:52:41.810 --> 00:52:43.660
So let's start on this side.

00:52:46.610 --> 00:52:48.900
- [Male Speaker] Hi, I live in Ukraine,

00:52:48.900 --> 00:52:51.023
have done so for the past 25 years.

00:52:52.290 --> 00:52:56.060
I'd like to ask you your view
on the situation in Ukraine

00:52:56.060 --> 00:52:57.990
with reference to two
things in particular.

00:52:57.990 --> 00:53:02.090
First, do the violations
of the Budapest Treaty

00:53:02.090 --> 00:53:04.660
fundamentally undermine
nuclear non-proliferation

00:53:04.660 --> 00:53:05.493
for the future?

00:53:06.410 --> 00:53:10.760
And second, the US with
Japan, which I believe

00:53:10.760 --> 00:53:14.960
would be favorable to this, I
know it would be, and the EU,

00:53:14.960 --> 00:53:16.630
helped to get the world buying some MIGA

00:53:16.630 --> 00:53:20.880
political risk insurance
program going for Ukraine again,

00:53:20.880 --> 00:53:22.490
so that there are gonna be investment

00:53:22.490 --> 00:53:23.630
from the rest of the world.

00:53:23.630 --> 00:53:27.630
You've done a fine job with
OPIC, but a lot of the world

00:53:27.630 --> 00:53:30.050
that was investing in Ukraine has stopped

00:53:30.050 --> 00:53:34.430
and it's produced a catastrophic
25% decline in the GDP.

00:53:34.430 --> 00:53:36.480
And this wasn't caused by Ukraine,

00:53:36.480 --> 00:53:38.220
it was caused by Ukraine's reliance

00:53:38.220 --> 00:53:41.000
on the security assurances
of the Budapest Treaty,

00:53:41.000 --> 00:53:44.240
based on which we gave
up 5000 nuclear weapons.

00:53:44.240 --> 00:53:49.240
So can the United States help with this?

00:53:51.260 --> 00:53:55.210
- Well, certainly, we
can and we are working

00:53:55.210 --> 00:53:58.030
to help Ukraine in many different ways,

00:53:58.030 --> 00:54:01.283
with its economic
recovery, just yesterday,

00:54:03.896 --> 00:54:06.933
I was saying we are
working to help Ukraine

00:54:08.860 --> 00:54:13.400
in various ways, including
with its economic recovery.

00:54:13.400 --> 00:54:18.400
Just yesterday in Kiev,
we announced the third

00:54:19.100 --> 00:54:22.290
one billion dollar US
government loan guarantee

00:54:23.130 --> 00:54:25.980
for the government in
Kiev, we are providing

00:54:25.980 --> 00:54:29.750
technical assistance, we're
providing military aid,

00:54:29.750 --> 00:54:33.520
we're providing various forms of security

00:54:33.520 --> 00:54:35.787
and economic support to the government,

00:54:35.787 --> 00:54:37.720
and the loan guarantees, of course,

00:54:37.720 --> 00:54:42.720
are not the sum total of
our economic assistance,

00:54:42.730 --> 00:54:47.070
but they're part and parcel,
we've also been very active

00:54:47.070 --> 00:54:50.890
with Europe and the international
financial institutions

00:54:50.890 --> 00:54:55.890
to try to provide the support
that Ukraine is now receiving

00:54:56.250 --> 00:54:59.450
through the international
financial institutions.

00:54:59.450 --> 00:55:03.300
So we are very much trying to support

00:55:03.300 --> 00:55:08.300
economic recovery in
Ukraine and its independence

00:55:11.440 --> 00:55:16.440
from its former dependence
on Russia and the East.

00:55:16.660 --> 00:55:21.660
So there is, there have
been some creative ideas

00:55:21.660 --> 00:55:24.620
and you may be referring
to some of the ideas

00:55:24.620 --> 00:55:26.360
that George Soros has talked about.

00:55:26.360 --> 00:55:27.820
- [Male Speaker] Correct.

00:55:27.820 --> 00:55:30.980
- And these are ones that we
have looked at very carefully,

00:55:30.980 --> 00:55:34.330
I've spoken myself to
Mr. Soros about this,

00:55:34.330 --> 00:55:37.680
as have many of my colleagues,
including Secretary Liu.

00:55:37.680 --> 00:55:42.680
His idea relies on a mechanism
within the European Union

00:55:42.880 --> 00:55:46.840
and their ability to activate a mechanism

00:55:46.840 --> 00:55:49.753
that could leverage additional
funds, and unfortunately,

00:55:50.880 --> 00:55:52.830
there are a number of
complexities with it,

00:55:52.830 --> 00:55:55.420
but the principal one
being that the Europeans

00:55:55.420 --> 00:55:58.140
have not been interested
in pursuing that option.

00:55:58.140 --> 00:55:59.330
- [Male Speaker] Yes, his way of doing it

00:55:59.330 --> 00:56:02.250
is not the only way, there are other ways.

00:56:02.250 --> 00:56:05.300
And as a lawyer in the private
sector there for 25 years,

00:56:05.300 --> 00:56:07.940
we have a lot of clients
that would like to come in

00:56:07.940 --> 00:56:09.820
and make a contribution to the economy,

00:56:09.820 --> 00:56:12.130
the private sector has been forgotten.

00:56:12.130 --> 00:56:14.803
He is correct, without
political risk insurance,

00:56:15.780 --> 00:56:20.460
when it gets to board approval
level, companies are afraid,

00:56:20.460 --> 00:56:23.130
with that, they will go forward with plans

00:56:23.130 --> 00:56:24.770
they would like to implement.

00:56:24.770 --> 00:56:28.070
And I personally have
actually, my law firm,

00:56:28.070 --> 00:56:30.380
a couple of billion dollars of investment

00:56:30.380 --> 00:56:33.320
that is presently frozen
that could be unblocked

00:56:33.320 --> 00:56:36.410
with political risk
insurance at the MIGA level,

00:56:36.410 --> 00:56:38.450
as has been done for
the West Bank and Gaza.

00:56:38.450 --> 00:56:43.150
That would work, Soros'
mechanism probably doesn't.

00:56:43.150 --> 00:56:45.729
- Well, I appreciate that and
I'll look into it, thank you.

00:56:45.729 --> 00:56:47.440
- [Male Speaker] Thank you.

00:56:47.440 --> 00:56:49.140
- [Mary] Yes, this gentleman here.

00:56:50.060 --> 00:56:53.453
- [Male Speaker] Thank you,
Ambassador Rice, and brother.

00:56:54.940 --> 00:56:56.240
- You can call him Johnny.

00:56:58.658 --> 00:56:59.817
- [Male Speaker] It was
very interesting to hear

00:56:59.817 --> 00:57:01.470
the personal side, to know that,

00:57:01.470 --> 00:57:06.470
behind that very informidable
government figure

00:57:07.670 --> 00:57:10.040
that you are, that there
is a very personal side,

00:57:10.040 --> 00:57:11.140
that's very important.

00:57:12.360 --> 00:57:15.560
I just want to, my question
has to do with Syria,

00:57:15.560 --> 00:57:18.210
but very quickly regarding
this gentleman, Ukraine.

00:57:18.210 --> 00:57:22.180
We represent a consortium of
students all over the world

00:57:22.180 --> 00:57:26.150
and many of them are in
Ukraine, and they asked me

00:57:26.150 --> 00:57:28.920
to ask you one question or
to let you know one thing,

00:57:28.920 --> 00:57:31.660
and that they are very
concerned about the fact

00:57:31.660 --> 00:57:34.750
that the general prosecutor
in Ukraine, they feel,

00:57:34.750 --> 00:57:39.750
is corrupt and is not really doing,

00:57:39.960 --> 00:57:42.520
making any progress in
terms of judicial reform,

00:57:42.520 --> 00:57:47.000
in terms of really reforming
the judiciary and reforming,

00:57:47.000 --> 00:57:49.500
and getting rid of a lot
of government figures,

00:57:49.500 --> 00:57:52.030
and this is what they feel
and they are just wondering

00:57:52.030 --> 00:57:53.880
if the United States could exert

00:57:53.880 --> 00:57:56.970
some kind of leverage or
at least look into the fact

00:57:56.970 --> 00:58:00.610
that this general prosecutor is very much

00:58:00.610 --> 00:58:03.710
not on the side of the Ukraine citizenry?

00:58:03.710 --> 00:58:07.030
But my main question has to do with Syria,

00:58:07.030 --> 00:58:12.030
and what I consider is not a
no fly zone, but a buffer zone.

00:58:14.280 --> 00:58:18.150
And the fact that we also
deal with Russian students,

00:58:18.150 --> 00:58:22.910
and they all seem to feel
that the Russian government

00:58:22.910 --> 00:58:27.560
might be very amenable to considering

00:58:27.560 --> 00:58:31.850
the establishment of a
buffer zone so that refugees,

00:58:31.850 --> 00:58:35.669
instead of having to
cross the Mediterranean,

00:58:35.669 --> 00:58:40.669
could rest or be settled in
this zone with, obviously,

00:58:41.900 --> 00:58:44.120
there's a lot of, you know,
there's a lot of problems

00:58:44.120 --> 00:58:46.560
with that, I realize, there's cost

00:58:46.560 --> 00:58:48.860
and then there's possibility of conflict,

00:58:48.860 --> 00:58:52.590
but if the Russian government
is willing to support this,

00:58:52.590 --> 00:58:56.040
and they feel it will
because the refugees,

00:58:56.040 --> 00:58:59.033
a lot of these refugees or
a lot of these terrorists,

00:59:00.430 --> 00:59:03.000
a lot of jihadists could
be part of the refugees

00:59:03.000 --> 00:59:04.483
and going back to Russia.

00:59:05.810 --> 00:59:09.810
So that I'm wondering why
the United States government

00:59:09.810 --> 00:59:14.050
has not pursued, or at
least outwardly pursued

00:59:14.050 --> 00:59:17.990
the establishment more of
a buffer zone in Syria?

00:59:17.990 --> 00:59:18.950
Thank you very much.

00:59:18.950 --> 00:59:22.300
- Well, thank you for the
question, it's a topical one.

00:59:22.300 --> 00:59:25.590
We have, at various
points during the course

00:59:25.590 --> 00:59:30.220
of the crisis in Syria, weighed the merits

00:59:30.220 --> 00:59:35.220
and risks of various forms of
zones, as we shorthand them,

00:59:36.180 --> 00:59:40.070
no fly zones, safe zones on the ground

00:59:40.070 --> 00:59:42.910
where civilians could be protected,

00:59:42.910 --> 00:59:44.460
I think that's what you mean.

00:59:44.460 --> 00:59:46.000
And then we've also looked at something

00:59:46.000 --> 00:59:48.450
that we've termed a buffer zone,

00:59:48.450 --> 00:59:50.690
which is not necessarily a safe zone

00:59:50.690 --> 00:59:52.920
with protection for
civilians on the ground,

00:59:52.920 --> 00:59:56.910
but some zone meant to be a bulwark

00:59:56.910 --> 01:00:01.910
so that people and weapons
and other malicious flows

01:00:02.710 --> 01:00:06.613
of goods and the like
can't continue to occur.

01:00:08.150 --> 01:00:09.890
In all honesty, these are options

01:00:09.890 --> 01:00:12.910
that we continue to examine.

01:00:12.910 --> 01:00:15.650
We have not ruled out any of them

01:00:17.530 --> 01:00:20.840
in a formal or permanent
way, but we've looked at them

01:00:20.840 --> 01:00:24.790
very very closely including,
again, very recently.

01:00:24.790 --> 01:00:29.023
And they are fraught with real
challenges and difficulties.

01:00:29.950 --> 01:00:34.210
They are incredibly resource
intensive to secure,

01:00:34.210 --> 01:00:38.260
so take your model of, let
me call it a safe zone,

01:00:38.260 --> 01:00:43.260
on let's say the northern or
southern border within Syria,

01:00:43.940 --> 01:00:48.133
either abutting Turkey
or abutting Jordan where,

01:00:49.450 --> 01:00:51.963
theoretically, civilians
might be protected.

01:00:54.316 --> 01:00:57.110
To enforce a safe zone effectively,

01:00:57.110 --> 01:01:01.840
you not only need planes in
the air, and the planes in

01:01:01.840 --> 01:01:04.810
the air are complicated because
the threat that most of the,

01:01:04.810 --> 01:01:09.463
the most proximate danger
to civilians is, in fact,

01:01:10.620 --> 01:01:13.070
not fixed wing aircraft, it's helicopters

01:01:13.070 --> 01:01:15.250
dropping barrel bombs that are harder

01:01:15.250 --> 01:01:18.850
to deal with in the context of an air cap.

01:01:18.850 --> 01:01:20.530
And then it's on the ground where you need

01:01:20.530 --> 01:01:23.250
literally tens of thousands of troops

01:01:23.250 --> 01:01:26.083
to hold and clear that territory.

01:01:27.230 --> 01:01:29.173
Where those troops would come from,

01:01:31.120 --> 01:01:35.810
whether weighing our
interests, you would use

01:01:35.810 --> 01:01:39.590
our air power and our
forces to protect folks

01:01:39.590 --> 01:01:43.040
on the ground and clearing out territory

01:01:43.040 --> 01:01:45.190
or whether you would use
them to go after ISIL

01:01:45.190 --> 01:01:47.290
because they are not the same mission

01:01:47.290 --> 01:01:49.810
is one of the tricky questions

01:01:49.810 --> 01:01:51.600
that policy makers have to weigh.

01:01:51.600 --> 01:01:54.140
A trade off where, if we were

01:01:54.140 --> 01:01:57.610
to employ additional resources,
would they best be utilized?

01:01:57.610 --> 01:02:00.560
A humanitarian mission, which
is the one you're describing,

01:02:03.469 --> 01:02:05.890
is not the same as a counter ISIL mission

01:02:05.890 --> 01:02:07.380
in the current context.

01:02:07.380 --> 01:02:09.620
There may be a point when they align,

01:02:09.620 --> 01:02:11.213
but they don't at the moment.

01:02:12.110 --> 01:02:15.637
And then you have the
question of to what extent

01:02:15.637 --> 01:02:18.280
does that actually improve
conditions on the ground?

01:02:18.280 --> 01:02:21.210
The major border crossings
are open to humanitarian aid,

01:02:21.210 --> 01:02:25.730
the aid is flowing, so particularly into

01:02:25.730 --> 01:02:28.480
the areas near the border
that we're talking about.

01:02:28.480 --> 01:02:31.690
So the incremental benefit to civilians

01:02:31.690 --> 01:02:33.850
our actual humanitarian experts

01:02:33.850 --> 01:02:36.180
do not think is very significant.

01:02:36.180 --> 01:02:38.560
And there's a real question as to whether

01:02:38.560 --> 01:02:40.140
the establishment of these zones

01:02:40.140 --> 01:02:43.510
would draw people back in or
actually cause them to flee

01:02:43.510 --> 01:02:45.700
for fear that they're gonna
be trapped in these zones

01:02:45.700 --> 01:02:48.410
where they'd rather have
freedom of movement,

01:02:48.410 --> 01:02:50.570
internally or externally.

01:02:50.570 --> 01:02:53.860
So there's a huge number
of complexities associated

01:02:53.860 --> 01:02:56.210
with this, which is not
to say these are options

01:02:56.210 --> 01:02:58.680
that are off the table, but
we are digging into them

01:02:58.680 --> 01:03:03.040
very deeply, and they are not as simple

01:03:03.040 --> 01:03:06.430
and obvious solutions as you
might hear by turning on,

01:03:06.430 --> 01:03:09.823
you know, CNN and listening to
what the pundits are saying.

01:03:10.950 --> 01:03:12.630
- [Mary] Thank you, and over here.

01:03:12.630 --> 01:03:15.540
- [Female Speaker] Hi,
sorry, I have laryngitis

01:03:15.540 --> 01:03:17.620
but I didn't wanna pass
up this opportunity

01:03:17.620 --> 01:03:20.680
to ask you a question, it
may be for both of you.

01:03:20.680 --> 01:03:24.720
One is, as a woman of
color, you're constantly

01:03:24.720 --> 01:03:27.220
in international settings where

01:03:27.220 --> 01:03:30.090
you are maybe the only
woman in the room or

01:03:30.090 --> 01:03:32.340
the only African American in the room,

01:03:32.340 --> 01:03:33.690
or well (background noise
drowns out speaker) but--

01:03:34.658 --> 01:03:36.120
(audience laughter)

01:03:36.120 --> 01:03:37.623
- Not always, but often.

01:03:40.084 --> 01:03:40.917
- [Female Speaker] I was just wondering

01:03:40.917 --> 01:03:43.410
how you handle yourself
in those situations

01:03:43.410 --> 01:03:46.420
where you might find sexism or some type

01:03:46.420 --> 01:03:50.743
of discrimination against
you because of who you are.

01:03:51.680 --> 01:03:53.390
And the other part of my question is,

01:03:53.390 --> 01:03:56.160
I teach at an inner city
high school here in New Haven

01:03:56.160 --> 01:04:00.123
at Hillhouse and it may be
more towards you, Johnny.

01:04:01.200 --> 01:04:05.080
What do you think we can
do at the local level

01:04:05.080 --> 01:04:09.610
to help our kids to like
experience things like this,

01:04:09.610 --> 01:04:12.940
to expose them to international,

01:04:12.940 --> 01:04:16.720
because they think very small,
they think very locally.

01:04:16.720 --> 01:04:21.720
And to get them to think
more outside of New Haven

01:04:22.560 --> 01:04:27.350
or their particular
situations and contribute,

01:04:27.350 --> 01:04:29.470
like you were talking about at the end,

01:04:29.470 --> 01:04:33.010
about there are so many
resources that we don't have,

01:04:33.010 --> 01:04:37.100
for whatever reason, that
America might be losing out

01:04:37.100 --> 01:04:38.940
on the potential that we have.

01:04:38.940 --> 01:04:40.910
And who knows, you know, maybe the person

01:04:40.910 --> 01:04:42.820
who has a cure for cancer
is a high school student

01:04:42.820 --> 01:04:44.830
in Hillhouse, but they may not have

01:04:44.830 --> 01:04:47.400
the same opportunities that others have.

01:04:47.400 --> 01:04:49.310
So if you could address that, and then--

01:04:49.310 --> 01:04:51.310
- Okay, could you all hear the question?

01:04:53.350 --> 01:04:57.510
So one was directed to me and
one was directed to Johnny.

01:04:57.510 --> 01:05:00.450
The one directed to me was, and I hope

01:05:00.450 --> 01:05:03.340
I don't do it misjustice, but essentially,

01:05:03.340 --> 01:05:07.000
as an African American
woman finding herself

01:05:07.000 --> 01:05:09.170
in various international settings,

01:05:09.170 --> 01:05:13.570
how do I deal with some,
being the only one or being

01:05:14.690 --> 01:05:16.390
certainly one of the few?

01:05:16.390 --> 01:05:21.313
And have I faced, you know,
discrimination and the like?

01:05:23.150 --> 01:05:26.340
I think the answer is,
you know, obviously,

01:05:26.340 --> 01:05:29.970
there are some
circumstances where I may be

01:05:29.970 --> 01:05:33.443
the only woman in the room
or the only person of color.

01:05:35.040 --> 01:05:38.310
But I'm representing the
United States of America.

01:05:38.310 --> 01:05:43.310
And I have found that, when I show up

01:05:44.520 --> 01:05:47.430
in whatever role I'm
in, even when I was in

01:05:47.430 --> 01:05:50.420
far more junior roles, as a representative

01:05:50.420 --> 01:05:52.020
of the United States of America,

01:05:52.020 --> 01:05:56.440
people primarily dealt
with me on that basis.

01:05:56.440 --> 01:06:01.440
And I can't pretend that there
haven't been occasions when,

01:06:02.600 --> 01:06:07.600
you know, people have made
flip comments or, you know,

01:06:09.560 --> 01:06:12.900
been somewhat condescending,
but for the most part,

01:06:12.900 --> 01:06:17.175
they don't have the space or the ability

01:06:17.175 --> 01:06:19.890
to do that or to dwell
on it because they know,

01:06:19.890 --> 01:06:22.830
at the end of the day,
that they have to deal,

01:06:22.830 --> 01:06:24.740
for better or for worse, with whatever

01:06:26.020 --> 01:06:28.970
the United States government
is coming or saying or doing.

01:06:28.970 --> 01:06:31.740
And that's the basis on which I have found

01:06:31.740 --> 01:06:36.740
that I'm interacting on
most, if not all occasions.

01:06:37.760 --> 01:06:42.750
And so I, to a great
extent, have not found that

01:06:42.750 --> 01:06:45.820
to be an impediment.

01:06:45.820 --> 01:06:48.340
Far more of an impediment, frankly,

01:06:48.340 --> 01:06:51.900
but still overwritten by this
fact that I was representing

01:06:51.900 --> 01:06:55.860
the US government, was
when I was 32 years old

01:06:55.860 --> 01:06:58.290
and the assistant secretary
of state for African affairs,

01:06:58.290 --> 01:07:02.030
and often the senior American
official going around

01:07:03.050 --> 01:07:05.840
representing the United
States, and it was my youth

01:07:05.840 --> 01:07:10.840
rather than my gender or my
race that was most noticed.

01:07:12.580 --> 01:07:16.520
And still not something
that, at the end of the day,

01:07:16.520 --> 01:07:18.283
got in the way of my doing my job.

01:07:19.370 --> 01:07:21.463
- I quickly say, relative to Hillhouse.

01:07:22.360 --> 01:07:27.360
Sure, you know, the easy answer
is to expose your students

01:07:29.030 --> 01:07:31.150
to what's happening over
here at Yale, then get them

01:07:31.150 --> 01:07:33.520
over here, invite them to
things like this and so forth.

01:07:33.520 --> 01:07:36.350
I think that's part of it, I'm
sure we can do more of that.

01:07:36.350 --> 01:07:38.270
But I also think the tougher
thing and the thing we need

01:07:38.270 --> 01:07:42.020
to do more of is take Yale over

01:07:42.020 --> 01:07:46.780
to them and meet them where they are.

01:07:46.780 --> 01:07:49.853
You know, I just, coincidentally,
when I was a student here,

01:07:51.170 --> 01:07:56.170
I started a, you know,
I got my first exposure

01:07:56.240 --> 01:08:01.240
to kind of, my first bug and
excitement around mentoring

01:08:03.096 --> 01:08:05.320
and the social sector, I
actually started a program

01:08:05.320 --> 01:08:08.390
over at Hillhouse High School,
this was back in the 80s,

01:08:08.390 --> 01:08:09.990
that was called the Young
Men's Leadership Forum,

01:08:09.990 --> 01:08:12.660
and we took, we brought
a bunch of Yale students

01:08:12.660 --> 01:08:17.660
over to Hillhouse every week
and we did study skills,

01:08:18.070 --> 01:08:20.900
mentoring, we did, you know,
a lot of work on the ground

01:08:20.900 --> 01:08:22.180
in the classrooms with those students

01:08:22.180 --> 01:08:26.090
and just to provide, most of
whom were teenage fathers,

01:08:26.090 --> 01:08:30.610
okay, and we just met them
where they were and we helped

01:08:30.610 --> 01:08:32.471
and we built relationships.

01:08:32.471 --> 01:08:35.950
And we were there, you
know, in their house.

01:08:35.950 --> 01:08:36.783
Now, I think that really
made the difference.

01:08:36.783 --> 01:08:41.100
And the other thing we did,
which is sort of that I think

01:08:41.100 --> 01:08:45.730
you could argue is the kind
of thing we could do more of,

01:08:45.730 --> 01:08:47.920
but just as a fun entrepreneurial thing,

01:08:47.920 --> 01:08:49.040
you know, for those of you who don't know,

01:08:49.040 --> 01:08:51.380
at least back then, Hillhouse had

01:08:51.380 --> 01:08:53.490
a great basketball tradition.

01:08:53.490 --> 01:08:58.010
And they would, when they
would play Wilbur Cross here,

01:08:58.010 --> 01:08:59.710
they would have more
people in Payne Whitney Gym

01:08:59.710 --> 01:09:01.230
than we would have when we were playing

01:09:01.230 --> 01:09:03.290
at the Yale basketball team.

01:09:03.290 --> 01:09:06.220
And so they thought they were
really good at basketball,

01:09:06.220 --> 01:09:08.620
so I brought (mumbles), I was
in the basketball team here,

01:09:08.620 --> 01:09:11.850
we went over to Hillhouse
and played, and brought,

01:09:11.850 --> 01:09:13.820
you know, when we were
seniors, played a game

01:09:13.820 --> 01:09:17.710
in their gym, Yale varsity
basketball versus them.

01:09:17.710 --> 01:09:21.300
And we had them introduce
it, you know, speak to,

01:09:21.300 --> 01:09:23.000
we had speakers, it was
just a fantastic sort of

01:09:23.000 --> 01:09:25.220
community event and
built a lot of bridges.

01:09:25.220 --> 01:09:26.890
Things like that, I mean,
that's way, you know,

01:09:26.890 --> 01:09:27.970
many many years ago.

01:09:27.970 --> 01:09:30.760
But it's just an example of things

01:09:30.760 --> 01:09:33.110
that we can do more of and
I'm sure we're doing a lot of,

01:09:33.110 --> 01:09:35.710
much already, that Peter could speak to,

01:09:35.710 --> 01:09:40.110
but filtering out ways to
bring the best of Yale to them

01:09:40.110 --> 01:09:42.750
in addition to thinking about exposure

01:09:42.750 --> 01:09:45.420
around them coming to us.

01:09:45.420 --> 01:09:47.220
- [Mary] Great, so who won the game?

01:09:47.220 --> 01:09:48.124
- We killed them.

01:09:48.124 --> 01:09:50.490
(audience laughter)

01:09:50.490 --> 01:09:51.760
- [Mary] Are there actual witnesses?

01:09:51.760 --> 01:09:55.550
- Yes, we killed them, and yes, there are.

01:09:55.550 --> 01:09:57.590
So Tim Shriver who's,
you know, the chairman

01:09:57.590 --> 01:10:01.470
in Special Olympics, was a
teacher over at Hillhouse then.

01:10:01.470 --> 01:10:04.410
And he will attest, he was my partner

01:10:04.410 --> 01:10:07.680
in building this initiative
that was very successful.

01:10:07.680 --> 01:10:12.120
And not only did we win but
out, wasn't me in this case,

01:10:12.120 --> 01:10:14.400
but one of our, one of my teammates

01:10:14.400 --> 01:10:17.630
won the dunk contest
against those guys, okay.

01:10:17.630 --> 01:10:21.490
So it was an eye opening
moment for Hillhouse students.

01:10:21.490 --> 01:10:22.710
(audience laughter)

01:10:22.710 --> 01:10:24.970
- [Mary] Yes, over here, please.

01:10:24.970 --> 01:10:26.500
- [Male Speaker] So there's
a little over a year left

01:10:26.500 --> 01:10:28.670
in President Obama's term,
and I'm just wondering,

01:10:28.670 --> 01:10:30.823
looking back and even
a little bit forward,

01:10:31.830 --> 01:10:35.120
is there such a thing
as an Obama doctrine,

01:10:35.120 --> 01:10:36.770
anything resembling that?

01:10:36.770 --> 01:10:38.680
And if that's not
something that's the case,

01:10:38.680 --> 01:10:41.573
is there something, is
there a Rice doctrine?

01:10:42.520 --> 01:10:45.270
- The Rice doctrine would
have to be the Obama doctrine

01:10:46.980 --> 01:10:49.453
for the next 15 months.

01:10:50.580 --> 01:10:51.413
No.

01:10:52.620 --> 01:10:57.620
You know, we've really
refrained from trying to distill

01:11:00.290 --> 01:11:02.350
our approach into a doctrine.

01:11:02.350 --> 01:11:06.650
I think if you understand
the way President Obama

01:11:06.650 --> 01:11:09.363
approaches foreign policy
and national security.

01:11:10.300 --> 01:11:15.150
He is both principled and pragmatic.

01:11:15.150 --> 01:11:18.920
And he would reject, as a matter

01:11:18.920 --> 01:11:23.920
of intellectual point of
view but also as a matter

01:11:24.070 --> 01:11:27.973
of practice, a one size fits all approach.

01:11:30.850 --> 01:11:33.203
So no, we don't have a doctrine.

01:11:34.250 --> 01:11:37.070
What he would say and
what he deeply believes

01:11:37.070 --> 01:11:41.440
is that American leadership is necessary

01:11:41.440 --> 01:11:45.620
and indispensable, that
it can be and must be

01:11:45.620 --> 01:11:48.520
a force for progress and for good,

01:11:48.520 --> 01:11:51.190
but that there are limits to what

01:11:51.190 --> 01:11:53.480
even the most powerful
nation on the planet

01:11:54.360 --> 01:11:58.810
can accomplish for other
people in their own countries.

01:11:58.810 --> 01:12:01.790
And at the end of the day, whether it's

01:12:01.790 --> 01:12:04.490
political transformation
or economic growth

01:12:06.333 --> 01:12:10.750
or even conflict resolution,
there are vital contributions

01:12:10.750 --> 01:12:13.410
that the United States and others

01:12:13.410 --> 01:12:15.790
in the international community,

01:12:15.790 --> 01:12:19.700
typically with American
leadership, can bring to bear,

01:12:19.700 --> 01:12:22.980
but at the end of the day,
there are certain fundamentals

01:12:22.980 --> 01:12:27.430
that have to be accomplished by and for

01:12:27.430 --> 01:12:30.400
the people of a given
country or a given region,

01:12:30.400 --> 01:12:34.500
whether it's fighting
corruption, cronyism,

01:12:34.500 --> 01:12:36.760
insisting on respect for human rights,

01:12:36.760 --> 01:12:41.660
the outside world can do a
great deal to call attention

01:12:41.660 --> 01:12:45.270
and demand certain
standards and performance,

01:12:45.270 --> 01:12:49.280
but at the end of the
day, it will not last

01:12:49.280 --> 01:12:54.280
and it will not be something
that is fundamentally enduring

01:12:57.500 --> 01:13:02.500
unless it is owned by the
local population, and that's,

01:13:02.860 --> 01:13:05.900
in many ways, the challenge
we're facing in Syria and Iraq,

01:13:05.900 --> 01:13:08.990
it's the challenge we're
facing in Afghanistan,

01:13:08.990 --> 01:13:11.680
it's the challenge that you could argue

01:13:11.680 --> 01:13:16.330
is at the root of many of the
most difficult global problems

01:13:16.330 --> 01:13:17.943
that we are wrestling with.

01:13:19.270 --> 01:13:20.880
- [Mary] Jackie.

01:13:20.880 --> 01:13:22.410
- [Jackie] Hi, thank you
so much, first of all,

01:13:22.410 --> 01:13:24.550
for sharing your insights with us.

01:13:24.550 --> 01:13:26.330
Obviously, many of us
are students in the room

01:13:26.330 --> 01:13:27.330
and we're figuring out what we want

01:13:27.330 --> 01:13:29.370
our priority specialty to be and how,

01:13:29.370 --> 01:13:30.630
and if we want those to relate

01:13:30.630 --> 01:13:32.530
to our careers after education.

01:13:32.530 --> 01:13:34.300
So I was just wondering,
since you hold degrees

01:13:34.300 --> 01:13:36.280
in different disciplines,
if you could talk about

01:13:36.280 --> 01:13:38.030
the values and interests
that took you from there

01:13:38.030 --> 01:13:39.180
to where you are today?

01:13:40.600 --> 01:13:42.430
- I wish I could tell you that, you know,

01:13:42.430 --> 01:13:47.390
there was a linear path
between, I was a history major.

01:13:47.390 --> 01:13:49.480
Johnny studied Latin American studies,

01:13:49.480 --> 01:13:52.193
and then I went on and did
international relations.

01:13:54.520 --> 01:13:56.290
I don't think so.

01:13:56.290 --> 01:14:01.290
My piece of advice, if I
have one, is very simple,

01:14:01.390 --> 01:14:03.310
and that's to follow your passion.

01:14:03.310 --> 01:14:07.570
Do whatever it is that
motivates you and excites you,

01:14:07.570 --> 01:14:09.740
whether you think it's gonna get you

01:14:09.740 --> 01:14:12.500
a good job coming out of college or not,

01:14:12.500 --> 01:14:17.140
do what you find
fascinating and motivating.

01:14:17.140 --> 01:14:18.610
And that's what you're gonna be good at

01:14:18.610 --> 01:14:21.700
and that's what will take you to the next

01:14:21.700 --> 01:14:23.510
and the next exciting thing.

01:14:23.510 --> 01:14:26.320
I never anticipated, when I was

01:14:26.320 --> 01:14:30.080
an undergraduate at
Stanford, that I would be

01:14:30.080 --> 01:14:31.780
in a career in international affairs,

01:14:31.780 --> 01:14:35.900
much less as national security
advisor or UN ambassador.

01:14:35.900 --> 01:14:40.337
That was not, I was going to
be a civil rights lawyer or,

01:14:40.337 --> 01:14:42.630
you know, a community activist.

01:14:42.630 --> 01:14:45.840
That's sort of how I thought of my future,

01:14:45.840 --> 01:14:50.840
and if I ever got a home
state that had voting rights

01:14:50.990 --> 01:14:54.080
as opposed to the District
of Columbia where I grew up,

01:14:54.080 --> 01:14:58.190
I thought maybe I might
run for public office.

01:14:58.190 --> 01:15:01.550
And I've ended up in a
completely different place.

01:15:01.550 --> 01:15:04.640
But the one thing I have
done is follow my heart

01:15:04.640 --> 01:15:08.820
and my head and do
things that I love to do,

01:15:08.820 --> 01:15:10.570
and that's never led me astray.

01:15:10.570 --> 01:15:12.300
So that's what I would say, Johnny--

01:15:12.300 --> 01:15:14.440
- The one thing I'll add to that is,

01:15:14.440 --> 01:15:15.770
while you're following your passion,

01:15:15.770 --> 01:15:18.390
work really hard at this
stage of your career

01:15:18.390 --> 01:15:22.040
to figure out what your genius gifts are,

01:15:22.040 --> 01:15:25.723
where you can be top 10,
top 20% in your peer group,

01:15:26.670 --> 01:15:29.570
what you do best and
what that translates into

01:15:29.570 --> 01:15:31.500
from a career standpoint,
because it's great

01:15:31.500 --> 01:15:33.190
to be focused on something
that you love, you know,

01:15:33.190 --> 01:15:36.319
if you wanna, you know, I
always share a story (mumbles),

01:15:36.319 --> 01:15:37.550
I wanna, you know, I wanna
be a recording artist.

01:15:37.550 --> 01:15:39.630
Well, you better be
able to sing, all right,

01:15:39.630 --> 01:15:41.700
if you can't sing, I don't
care how passionate you are

01:15:41.700 --> 01:15:43.220
about music, you ain't gonna make

01:15:43.220 --> 01:15:44.470
any money doing that, right.

01:15:44.470 --> 01:15:47.852
So focus on, you know, the combined,

01:15:47.852 --> 01:15:49.570
the people who get the farthest,

01:15:49.570 --> 01:15:54.479
people who realize their
potential, we found they combine

01:15:54.479 --> 01:15:56.700
that sweet spot, that lane, which is

01:15:56.700 --> 01:15:59.270
what you're passionate about
and what your, you know,

01:15:59.270 --> 01:16:01.450
where your genius gifts are.

01:16:01.450 --> 01:16:04.150
Because what you need to focus
on at this stage early on

01:16:04.150 --> 01:16:06.850
is being a high performer.

01:16:06.850 --> 01:16:08.520
And if you're a high, you're more likely

01:16:08.520 --> 01:16:09.500
to be a high performer if you're focusing

01:16:09.500 --> 01:16:11.050
on what you're passionate
about, but you're most likely

01:16:11.050 --> 01:16:12.130
to be a high performer
if you're really good

01:16:12.130 --> 01:16:13.250
at what you're doing.

01:16:13.250 --> 01:16:15.100
And being a high performer
will position you

01:16:15.100 --> 01:16:17.496
to develop the
relationships that you will,

01:16:17.496 --> 01:16:19.560
that will then catapult
your career that I think

01:16:19.560 --> 01:16:22.007
everyone here who's more,
you know, tenured there

01:16:22.007 --> 01:16:25.100
who could speak to about
how important that is.

01:16:25.100 --> 01:16:27.490
- So I'm afraid we're
actually out of time,

01:16:27.490 --> 01:16:31.290
I feel very badly for
these, your two lines

01:16:31.290 --> 01:16:33.130
that waited so patiently.

01:16:33.130 --> 01:16:35.210
Can we please give Ambassador Rice

01:16:35.210 --> 01:16:36.688
and John Rice anther round--

01:16:36.688 --> 01:16:38.938
(applause)

01:16:46.344 --> 01:16:47.177
Thank you.

